WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.486
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.949 --> 00:00:15.490
Good afternoon,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.210
and welcome to the
second [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.145
presentation by the
Distinguished Speakers

00:00:20.145 --> 00:00:21.780
Series [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.570
We are honored to have
with us today Steve Jobs,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.820
the president and
chief executive officer

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.010
of NeXT Computer Corporation.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.409
Steve has consistently been
one of the computer industry's

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:33.950
foremost entrepreneurs.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.000
Along with partner
Steve Wozniak,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.580
their entrance into
the industry was

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.870
by the traditional
entrepreneurial route

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.827
of building computers
in a garage.

00:00:42.827 --> 00:00:44.910
[INAUDIBLE] auspicious
start, however, [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.310
the creation of Apple,
a formidable challenge

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.025
to IBM in the personal
computer market.

00:00:50.025 --> 00:00:52.220
A true visionary,
Steve was known

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.600
at an early age for
his belief that a PC

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.749
could be put on every desk and
made simple enough for use.

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:00.540
Educationally, he had
the right credentials

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.760
to make it big in the
computer industry,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.761
because he dropped
out of Reed College.

00:01:04.761 --> 00:01:06.375
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:06.375 --> 00:01:10.090
[INAUDIBLE] and the
growing corporate culture

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:13.230
diverged, both eventually
left Apple to establish

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.120
their entrepreneurial roots.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.330
In 1985, Steve founded
another computer company,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.500
NeXT Corporation, to design a
revolutionary computer called

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.970
the Cube.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.950
Targeting the high end
workstation market,

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.590
NeXT has found the going
tough against [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.370
competitors, such
as Sun Microsystems.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:36.990
However, with 1991 revenues
hitting $127 million,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.330
business has grown
substantially.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.340
With the backing
of Ross Perot, NeXT

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.740
shouldn't be short of
funds in the future.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.339
Or in a more unlikely
scenario, a friendly year

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:47.130
in the White House.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.690
The current plans
for NeXT computer

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.410
involve a public offering,
possibly in the next 14 months.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.260
We hope to hear today just what
a good investment that should

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.060
be.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.390
Steve has consistently been
recognized for his contribution

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.450
to America's largest
high tech industry.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.419
Most recently, he was appointed
to the president's Export

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.726
Council last August,
with the role

00:02:11.726 --> 00:02:14.290
to advise on government
policies and programs that

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.350
affect trade performance.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.960
Outside of work, Steve is
married to Laurene, previously

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.120
an MBA student at
Stanford University,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.980
and also a sister of one of
our classmates, Brad Powell.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.215
The couple met when Steve
gave a similar speech

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:31.620
to this afternoon's at Stanford,
days before the Bay Area

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.640
earthquake.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.130
Let's hope that history
doesn't repeat itself.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.700
Ladies and gentlemen, we are
extremely grateful for Steve

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.470
to giving up his time to
be with us this afternoon.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.060
Could you please
give a warm welcome

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.426
to our distinguished
guest, Steve Jobs.

00:02:47.426 --> 00:02:50.877
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:05.210
Thank you.

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.550
Hi.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.650
I guess we get to spend an
hour or so together today.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.740
And most of the time,
I wanted to spend just

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.198
talking about what
you want to talk

00:03:13.198 --> 00:03:14.590
and answering some questions.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:17.440
But I thought if you wanted,
I'd take about 10 minutes or 15

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.470
minutes upfront and tell
you what we're doing at NeXT

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.830
and why the world might need
another computer company.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:27.111
Is that something you guys
think about these things?

00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:27.610
OK.

00:03:31.087 --> 00:03:33.170
I thought I'd tell you
about some of our mistakes.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.020
Maybe that would be more useful.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.460
We have a lot of scar tissue.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.540
There is a really
interesting book

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.830
that was written by a guy
named Paul Strassmann.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.800
And Paul has one of the more
interesting jobs in the planet.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.840
He's the Chief
Information Officer--

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.260
CIO of a very large organization
called the Pentagon.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:58.411
And they really
understand software there.

00:03:58.411 --> 00:04:00.910
I had a conversation with him
not too long ago, and he said,

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.910
the lesson from the Gulf War
was that the best software

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.560
will win the war.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.360
And so they're trying
to do a lot of work

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.090
in the software area.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.530
He wrote a book,
though, before he

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.200
got this job called The
Business Value of Computers.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.613
It's rather thick, and it's
not good bedtime reading.

00:04:16.613 --> 00:04:18.279
But you can plow
through it, and there's

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.170
some incredible stuff in it.

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:24.640
And he asked two
questions in particular.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.000
One was, he surveyed
a bunch of companies

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.070
from not very successful
all the way up

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.840
through really successful.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.950
And there's somebody
taking notes here.

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:40.150
He asked how much they spent
on information technology

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:41.740
as a percentage of revenues.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.750
And he got a very
counter-intuitive answer,

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:43.900
right?

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.700
You'd think that either the
really successful companies

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.590
would either spend
more or less than

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:50.380
the not-successful companies,
depending on your theory.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:51.940
But it was exactly the same.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.430
They all spent
about 2% of revenues

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.100
on information technology.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.470
And he found this curious, and
so he asked another question.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.420
How did they spend their money?

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.140
And he found out that the
really successful ones--

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:08.980
actually, let's start with
the not-so-successful ones.

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.890
As success increases
and dollars increase,

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.910
he found out that the
not-so-successful ones spent

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.920
the majority of their money
on management productivity,

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.050
and the more
successful ones spent

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:27.700
the majority of their money
on operational productivity

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.000
applications.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.426
Now, this was not very
pleasant for me to read,

00:05:32.426 --> 00:05:34.300
because I spent the
first 10 years of my life

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:38.170
on management productivity,
which was PCs.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.790
PCs and Macs never attacked
operational productivity.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.650
They just attacked
management productivity.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:45.310
Why is that?

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.670
Because you can't go down
to your local computer store

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:52.480
and buy an app that will
help you do stock trading,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.250
or will help you run a
hospital, or will help you

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:59.960
in whatever operational
part of your business

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.331
you want to automate.

00:06:01.331 --> 00:06:03.080
Unless you're a very,
very small business,

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.510
then you can run some
accounting packages.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.250
But other than that, if you
were a medium-sized or large

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.260
business, these
things never attacked

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.090
operational productivity.

00:06:12.591 --> 00:06:16.460
So we zoom out and we
say, how have people

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.874
attacked operational
productivity

00:06:17.874 --> 00:06:19.040
with information technology?

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.239
Well, in the '60s, they
bought a mainframe,

00:06:21.239 --> 00:06:23.780
and they got some terminals and
a bunch of COBOL programmers,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.400
and they wrote a few apps.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.880
And most of them were
kind of back-room apps.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.852
And it sort of worked
for the very few

00:06:30.852 --> 00:06:32.060
that could afford to do this.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:35.300
In the '70s, they got a
mainframe and some terminals,

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.800
and they did the same thing.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.260
And a few of them got a few
mini computers and terminals

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:40.760
and tried to do it
a little cheaper.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.800
In the '80s, nothing changed.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.750
Mainframe and terminals,
minis and terminals.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:48.360
Until maybe about two
or three years ago.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.520
What happened two
or three years ago

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.310
was that the front
office started

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:58.740
to realize that they needed
operational apps so bad

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.650
that they couldn't depend
on the MIS folks anymore.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.520
They started taking life
into their own hands

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.840
and sometimes working with the
MIS folks to start downsizing

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.820
and getting some
servers and running

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.850
some industry-standard
databases like Sybase or Oracle

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.490
in the servers, and making
a little local area network,

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.710
and getting maybe
some Sun Workstations,

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.690
and spending about
two years writing

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.260
some mission-critical
operational applications.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:29.160
Like trading apps for Wall
Street, perfect example.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.810
And it kind of worked.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.330
And the reason that
they needed to do this

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:36.060
was because more and more,
they were discovering

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.620
that things like new
products required a custom

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.660
operational application.

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.240
An example, if you're
in financial services

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.900
and you come up
with a new product,

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:48.750
it's only three things.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.070
It's an idea, it's
a sales force,

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:55.650
and it's a custom app
to bang on databases

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.866
to make the product real,
to do the mortgage swaps

00:07:58.866 --> 00:08:00.240
or whatever it is
you want to do.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.510
Without the app, you
don't have a product.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:07.710
And so there has been an
increasing buildup of demand

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.790
from the front parts
of corporations

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:15.000
to create more and more and
more of these operational

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.630
applications.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:18.780
And I think it's going
to get to the point

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.370
where this becomes
fairly clear that this

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.450
is the next big revolution
in desktop computing,

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:32.350
is to attack the
operational productivity.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:34.110
And as we start to
re-engineer the way

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:38.297
we do things, to automate a lot
of this in custom applications.

00:08:38.297 --> 00:08:40.130
Sounds a little strange
now, to most people.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:42.734
Sounds like desktop
publishing in 1985.

00:08:42.734 --> 00:08:44.150
Nobody knew what
it was, everybody

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:46.730
thought it was kind of a strange
vertical thing over there.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.750
But my guess is it's
pretty horizontal.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:53.410
And we're attacking
vertical markets now

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:56.320
that know they want this.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.790
And it's going extremely well.

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:01.690
Sun is the only company that's
really had any success at this,

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.540
and we're knocking
them out of the box.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.460
Because we came up
with the software

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:09.430
called NeXTSTEP which
lets you build apps

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:12.970
five to 10 times faster than
anything anyone's ever seen.

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:15.370
And after you build them,
they're deployable and usable

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:16.870
by mere mortals,
because it's really

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:18.400
easy to use, this computer.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.220
And you can interoperate
your custom apps seamlessly

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.510
with a bunch of off-the-shelf
productivity apps.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:27.062
So we go to these companies
that use Suns and take

00:09:27.062 --> 00:09:29.020
two years to write their
apps-- or are thinking

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.030
about using Suns, and
they can write their apps

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.130
in about 90 days on a NeXT.

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.260
Now, if you're on
Wall Street and you

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:39.130
can create a new product in
90 days versus your competitor

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.290
in two years, that's
eight new products

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.660
you can field for
their every one.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.090
And you can start to see the
competitive advantage that

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.400
can be created this way.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.370
Now, we had no idea that
we were any good at this

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.490
when we started NeXT.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.700
A lot of times you don't know
what your competitive advantage

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:02.292
is when you launch
a new product.

00:10:02.292 --> 00:10:03.750
Let me give you
historical example.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.926
When we created-- how
many of you guys use Macs?

00:10:05.926 --> 00:10:06.800
Anybody?

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:08.303
Good.

00:10:08.303 --> 00:10:09.570
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:12.070
How many of you
have seen a NeXT?

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.020
Oh, how many of you use a NeXT?

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:15.980
Oh, that's not so bad.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.200
We'd like to change
that ratio a little bit.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:21.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.610
We're on the right track.

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:28.720
When we did the
Macintosh, we never

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.380
anticipated desktop publishing
when we created the Mac.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.180
Sounds funny, because
that turned out

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:36.700
to be the Mac's compelling
advantage, right?

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.645
The thing that it did not one
and a half or two times better

00:10:39.645 --> 00:10:41.770
than everything else, but
four or five times better

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:44.500
than anything else, where
you had to have one.

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:46.030
We never anticipated it.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:48.324
We anticipated bitmap
displays and laser printers,

00:10:48.324 --> 00:10:50.740
but we never thought about
page maker, that whole industry

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:52.510
really coming down
to the desktop.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.880
Maybe we weren't smart enough.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.910
But we were smart enough
to see it start to happen

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.660
nine to 12 months later.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:04.580
And we changed our entire
marketing and business strategy

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:08.420
to focus on desktop publishing,
and it became the Trojan horse

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.770
that eventually got the
Mac into corporate America,

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:13.311
where it could show its owners
all the other wonderful things

00:11:13.311 --> 00:11:14.720
it could do.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.440
Likewise, when we
created NeXTSTEP,

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.750
this revolutionary
object-oriented software

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:23.680
that we have, our
target customer

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.090
coming from the PC world, where
shrinkwrapped apps were king,

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:30.130
was Lotus and Adobe
and WordPerfect

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.110
and all the shrinkwrapped
apps developers.

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:34.861
And the purpose was to let them
create their apps five to 10

00:11:34.861 --> 00:11:36.610
times faster for these
shrinkwrapped apps.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:37.160
And it worked.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:38.868
We have a ton of
shrinkwrapped apps, now.

00:11:38.868 --> 00:11:41.720
Best of breed in
almost every category.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:45.350
But it wasn't
until early in '91,

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:47.600
early last year, a
little over a year ago,

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.190
that some really big
companies came to us and said,

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.640
you don't understand
what you've got.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.900
The same software that allows
Lotus to create their apps five

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.840
to 10 times faster
is letting us build

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:03.440
our in-house mission-critical
apps five to 10 times faster.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:06.800
And this is the biggest
problem we've had.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.130
This is a huge problem for
every big company, and almost

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:11.596
all medium-sized
companies, and you

00:12:11.596 --> 00:12:13.970
have a solution in your hands,
and you dummies don't even

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:15.392
know it.

00:12:15.392 --> 00:12:17.600
And it took them about three
months before we finally

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:19.220
heard it.

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:22.430
And then last summer, we changed
our whole sales and marketing

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.530
strategy around
to focus on that.

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:26.480
And it's taken
off like a rocket.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:30.980
And we grew about 4x last year,
and probably grow about 2x

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:32.240
this year.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.260
And our customer list
is now very, very strong

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:37.380
and growing like crazy.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.700
We just got back from spending a
few days in DC and in New York.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:44.640
And we're talking to
customers we only dreamed

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.290
of talking to a year ago.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:48.910
So that's what we do.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:54.590
And our arch enemy Sun,
they want to kill us.

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:55.430
Which is good.

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:57.570
They should try to do
that as soon as possible,

00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:59.600
because the sooner they
do it, the cheaper it

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:01.330
will be for them.

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:02.974
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.460
I think it's gone past the
point where it's possible.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:11.180
And the greatest
thing is, hardware

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:12.680
churns every 18 months.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:16.250
It's pretty impossible to
get a sustainable competitive

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:19.370
advantage from hardware.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:22.010
If you're lucky, you can make
something one and a half or two

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:23.870
times as good as your
competitor, which

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:26.960
probably isn't enough to be
quite a competitive advantage.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.670
And it only lasts
for six months.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:32.780
But software seems to take
a lot longer for people

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:34.760
to catch up with.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:36.830
I watched Microsoft
take eight or nine

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.380
years to catch up with
the Mac, and it's arguable

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:41.630
whether they've even caught up.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.410
It takes a long time.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.530
And we think that
the soonest we're

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:49.080
going to have a true competitor
is probably four to five years.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.820
So we've got that amount
of time to grow ourselves

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:55.310
a $1 to $2 billion
company so that we

00:13:55.310 --> 00:13:56.840
can compete with them on scale.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:59.300
See, today we can't
compete with them on scale.

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:01.310
We never have as many
salespeople as they do,

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:02.210
as Sun does.

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:04.320
We don't have the ad
budgets that they do.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:07.289
So we've got to have
a better product.

00:14:07.289 --> 00:14:09.080
And I hope we always
have a better product,

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:09.877
and I think we can.

00:14:09.877 --> 00:14:11.960
But I'd also like to be
able to at least give them

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.680
a run for their money on scale.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.740
So we've got the next
three to four years

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:18.290
to run really fast,
so that by the time

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.780
they even get close to
having a competitive product,

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:22.730
we're at a large enough
scale to where we

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:25.260
can start to compete with them.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.747
And that's what we're doing
with our lives right now,

00:14:27.747 --> 00:14:29.330
spending a lot of
time with customers,

00:14:29.330 --> 00:14:32.240
spending a lot of time
making NeXTSTEP better,

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.130
and that kind of thing.

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:38.460
So that's the strategic
basis of what we do.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:39.793
Does that make any sense to you?

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:47.110
Have you run across the concept
of sort of operational custom

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:49.085
applications at all?

00:14:49.085 --> 00:14:51.460
I mean, most of you have come
from companies where you've

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:53.170
had work experience, right?

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:54.520
And you've all done that?

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.540
So do you have this problem in
the companies you come from,

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.280
of a lot of pressure to write
these operational custom

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:03.460
applications and hardly anything
coming out of the spigot

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:07.840
to satisfy this thirst?

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.600
How many of you
from Wall Street?

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:11.370
Good.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:12.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:17.260
Good How many of you from
manufacturing companies?

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:18.280
Excellent.

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:19.808
Where are the rest of you from?

00:15:19.808 --> 00:15:21.798
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:22.298 --> 00:15:24.430
Consulting.

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:26.410
How many from consulting?

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:27.980
Oh, that's bad.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.608
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:32.695 --> 00:15:35.080
A mind is too
important to waste.

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:38.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:39.568
You should do something.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.390
Why is that bad?

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.910
A consultant can come into a
company and use your system,

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:50.006
and basically build
their applications

00:15:50.006 --> 00:15:52.235
in predictably short
amounts of time,

00:15:52.235 --> 00:15:54.030
and show them a working product.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.530
The only consultants I've seen
that I think are truly useful

00:15:56.530 --> 00:16:00.080
are the ones that help
us sell our computers.

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.680
No seriously, I don't
think there's anything

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:04.690
inherently evil in consulting.

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.015
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:13.840
I think that without
owning something,

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:15.860
over an extended
period of time--

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.920
like, a few years--

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:20.710
where one has a chance
to take responsibility

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:23.050
for one's
recommendations, where one

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:26.860
has to see one's recommendations
through all action stages

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.110
and accumulate scar
tissue for the mistakes

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:33.850
and pick oneself up off the
ground and dust oneself off,

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:39.310
one learns a fraction
of what one can.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:40.810
Coming in and making
recommendations

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:46.750
and not owning the results,
not owning the implementation,

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:52.790
I think is a fraction
of the value,

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:54.710
and a fraction of
the opportunity

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.730
to learn and get better.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:03.170
And so you do get a broad cut at
companies, but it's very thin.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:06.166
It's like a picture of a--

00:17:06.166 --> 00:17:07.790
I'm a vegetarian, so
I won't use steak.

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:11.162
But it's like a
picture of a banana.

00:17:11.162 --> 00:17:12.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:15.050
You might get a very
accurate picture,

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:17.329
but it's only two dimensional.

00:17:17.329 --> 00:17:21.920
And without the experience
of actually doing it,

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.520
you never get three dimensional.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.160
So you might have a lot
of pictures on your walls.

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:26.369
You can show it off
to your friends.

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:28.077
You can say look, I've
worked in bananas,

00:17:28.077 --> 00:17:30.620
I've worked in peaches,
I've worked in grapes.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:34.430
But you never really taste it.

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:37.870
And that's what I think.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:40.270
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:48.060
You're also a variable expense.

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.710
And in hard times,
you find yourself.

00:17:52.662 --> 00:17:55.180
You find yourself
variable, right?

00:17:55.180 --> 00:17:57.860
If it's the software that's
going to make or break

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:00.110
your company, how come you're
putting out on platform.

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:01.589
Why don't you put it on Sun's?

00:18:01.589 --> 00:18:03.070
They have a much larger base.

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:04.757
Right, very good question.

00:18:04.757 --> 00:18:06.340
I'm going to generalize
your question.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:11.400
Why don't we just become
a software company, right?

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:12.682
That's a very good question.

00:18:12.682 --> 00:18:13.640
It's a subtle question.

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.765
I'm going to try to go
through some several things,

00:18:15.765 --> 00:18:19.820
and I'm sorry if I jump around.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.430
We got a lot of
requests from customers

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.730
last year that they would
love to see NeXTSTEP

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:28.580
on other platforms,
and primarily,

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.940
Intel-based platforms
like the 486.

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:34.440
So we decided to do just that.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.620
And we have ported
NeXTSTEP to the 486,

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.060
and we're finishing it now.

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:41.630
And it will ship in the
September, October timeframe.

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:45.890
And it's exactly the same stuff
we run in our own computer.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:48.890
Same app, same user
interface, same training,

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:50.630
same development environment.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:52.730
And we're going to
sell it for $9.95,

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.610
and we're OEMing it to a bunch
of companies whose names you'll

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:57.800
recognize quite easily,
and OEMing it to them

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:00.410
at a much cheaper price.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:05.370
And everybody's coming out
of the woodwork to help us.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:06.920
We're getting help from Novell.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:08.711
We're getting help from
all the developers.

00:19:08.711 --> 00:19:10.710
Intel's really helping us.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.430
And they really
want us to succeed.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.211
Why is that?

00:19:15.211 --> 00:19:15.710
Right.

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:19.370
They all want to make sure that
there's a choice out there,

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.220
and they're all really
scared about Microsoft.

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:24.170
And they see NeXTSTEP
as the only thing

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:25.640
on the horizon
that can challenge

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:29.070
Microsoft in system software
for the next several years.

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:32.420
So we're enjoying a lot of
help, and boy we need it,

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:34.080
so that's good.

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.600
Now, we've also had
a lot of requests

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.000
from companies to port
NeXTSTEP to other platforms.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.790
And we're talking to some of
those companies right now.

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:44.640
Now, we've got a lot of
requests from Sun customers

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:45.840
the port NeXTSTEP to Sun.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:46.910
So a lot of them
are saying, look, we

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:48.030
may not want to
buy them anymore,

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:49.340
but we already bought
500, and we don't

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:50.190
want to throw them in the bay.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:51.690
So can we put your
software in them?

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.590
Because Sun's falling
behind in software.

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:57.295
Now, Sun says they'd rather
stick needles in their eyes

00:19:57.295 --> 00:19:58.170
than help us do this.

00:19:58.170 --> 00:19:59.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:02.130
That's a quote.

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:09.720
And so we're evaluating
right now, which

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:14.100
will be worse for Sun, if we
port it or if we don't port it?

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:18.080
And since we're fairly
customer-driven,

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:20.600
we'll probably end up doing
what the customers ask us to do,

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:22.800
because we want to
make them happy.

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:27.780
Now, this leads one
into the question,

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:29.430
should we just be
a software company?

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.640
And we think the answer is no.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:35.960
We think we should be a software
company and a hardware company.

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:39.620
In making the decision
to put NeXTSTEP

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:42.320
on more than our own
platforms, we clearly

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:44.600
decided that we will
sell less than 100%

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.980
of the NeXTSTEP hardware.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.340
However, we think that
the marketplace will grow,

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:53.450
and we will sell more
absolute hardware.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:55.880
And secondly, the charter
of our hardware division

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:58.054
is to make the best
NeXTSTEP hardware.

00:20:58.054 --> 00:21:00.720
Might not be the cheapest, might
not be this, might not be that.

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.230
But we think all in all,
we can make the best stuff.

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:06.050
And I would love
at nothing better

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:08.450
than if someday we
only sold 20% or 25%

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:09.920
of the NeXTSTEP hardware.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:12.350
But I still think that's a
billion-dollar-plus hardware

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.490
business.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:16.870
And I'll get into one other
hardware-related reason

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:17.370
in a minute.

00:21:23.074 --> 00:21:24.990
There are some things I
can't talk about here.

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:37.000
In addition to that, if you look
at how we sell our computers

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.150
right now, we have a
sales force in the US

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:43.852
of about 130
professionals in the field

00:21:43.852 --> 00:21:46.150
out selling NeXT computers.

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:52.900
They spend 90% of their time
selling NeXTSTEP software,

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:56.977
and then 10% of their
time selling the hardware.

00:21:56.977 --> 00:21:58.810
In other words, if they
can get the customer

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:00.922
to buy into NeXTSTEP,
then they're

00:22:00.922 --> 00:22:03.130
going to sell the hardware,
because right now we have

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.210
the only hardware it runs on.

00:22:05.210 --> 00:22:09.370
So they are out there
selling NeXTSTEP right now.

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:12.610
And this is what is
required to launch

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:14.710
a new innovative product.

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:17.020
The current
distribution channels

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:20.200
for the computer industry
over the last several years

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:24.040
have lost their ability
to create demand.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:26.950
They can fulfill demand,
but they can't create it.

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:28.446
If a new product
comes out, you're

00:22:28.446 --> 00:22:30.820
lucky if you can find somebody
at the computer store that

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:33.730
even knows how to demo it.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.730
So the more innovative
the product is,

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:38.530
the more revolutionary
it is and not

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:42.280
just an incremental improvement,
the more you're stuck.

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:44.890
Because the existing channel
is only fulfilling demand.

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:46.870
Matter of fact, it's
getting so bad, that it's

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:50.110
getting wiped out, because there
are more efficient channels

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:54.310
to fulfill demand, like the
telephone and Federal Express.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:58.370
So we're seeing the channel
become condensed on its way

00:22:58.370 --> 00:23:00.700
to I think just telebusiness.

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:04.280
So how does one bring
innovation to the marketplace?

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:06.400
We believe the only
way we know how

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:08.500
to do it right now is with
the direct sales force,

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:10.780
out there in front of
customers showing them

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:13.570
the products in the environment
of their own problems,

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:15.130
and discussing how
those problems can

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:17.940
be mated with these solutions.

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:20.460
A software-only
company could never

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:23.370
afford to field a
direct sales force.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:27.420
With average selling prices
of $500 a software package,

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:32.600
you could never afford 130
professionals in the field.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:37.700
With an average selling
price of $5,000, you can.

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:39.260
And that's why I
don't think we're

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:45.180
going to see any more systems
software companies succeed.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:49.410
I don't think it's possible
to fund the efforts to educate

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:54.870
the market about a revolutionary
product with ASPs that low.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:57.000
And if it's not a
revolutionary product,

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:59.440
I don't think the
company can succeed.

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:01.980
So our strategy
has been that we've

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:06.240
got to be a hardware company
in order to make our software

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:07.205
business succeed.

00:24:07.205 --> 00:24:09.330
And we think we can do
really well at both of them.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:13.820
I know that's a long answer,
but it's a complex problem too.

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:17.780
Yes?

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:21.245
[INAUDIBLE]

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.790
Sure.

00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:41.100
If you ask us who
our competitors are,

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:43.560
we'll say really three things.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:47.330
One is Sun's Solaris software,
the other is Microsoft,

00:24:47.330 --> 00:24:48.985
and the third is Taligent.

00:24:48.985 --> 00:24:50.840
Let's take them in that order.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:55.537
Sun for a while had a
software value added,

00:24:55.537 --> 00:24:57.620
because they had the best
Unix in the marketplace.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:00.036
But the market's moved way
beyond that, and unfortunately,

00:25:00.036 --> 00:25:01.099
Sun hasn't.

00:25:01.099 --> 00:25:03.390
So their software's falling
further and further behind,

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:06.120
and while we take
them very seriously,

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.480
we don't think Solaris is
going to be much competition.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:12.860
It doesn't have an object
in it, and it's pretty much

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:15.020
what they have today.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:18.870
Microsoft is doing NT, which
is their sort of second attempt

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:20.390
that a Unix wannabe.

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:22.010
And that's great,
and I think it will

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:24.290
be better than the last
one, which was OS/2.

00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:26.930
But fundamentally, it's
just an operating system.

00:25:26.930 --> 00:25:28.990
It's better plumbing
for Windows.

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:30.680
That's a good way
of thinking of it.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:33.350
Unfortunately, you're
still stuck with Windows

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:37.220
in all of its glory, including
the worst development

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:39.410
environment that's
ever been invented.

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:41.870
And so we don't think
that this is really

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:44.646
going to present a challenge to
what we're going after, which

00:25:44.646 --> 00:25:46.020
are these
mission-critical custom

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:48.890
apps, because the development
environment is horrendous,

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:51.440
it's not object-oriented, and
even with the better plumbing,

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:54.440
we think it will be widely
rejected for what we do.

00:25:57.770 --> 00:26:00.200
In terms of Taligent,
Taligent represents

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:03.600
the first true competitor
that we could have.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:07.040
They're going to ship
a product around 1995,

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.630
if they execute to their plans.

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:12.710
And I think if they do
execute to their plans

00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:14.840
and work really hard,
by about the mid '90s,

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:17.500
they'll have roughly
what we have today.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:19.330
And that's not meant as a joke.

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:21.220
It still means we
have to run very hard,

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:24.832
because they will have a lot
of resources at their disposal.

00:26:24.832 --> 00:26:27.040
I think there's a lot of
questions as to whether they

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:28.210
will ever ship a product.

00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:31.480
I think they're a few years away
from having something running,

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:32.320
unquote.

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:34.270
But we take them very seriously.

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:36.430
Now, they've helped
us enormously,

00:26:36.430 --> 00:26:39.580
because they've blessed
object-oriented programming.

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.080
And right now,
we're the only folks

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:44.270
that have it, and will be for
the next three or four years.

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:47.530
So if we can't
compete with Taligent,

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:50.500
it's probably because we've
shot ourselves in the foot.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.180
You couldn't ask for
something better.

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:56.320
IBM and Apple saying, these
guys are right on track,

00:26:56.320 --> 00:27:00.100
and come back in five years
and we'll have it too.

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:01.270
It's great.

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:03.880
And so the ball's in our court.

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:10.235
Yes, in the back.

00:27:10.235 --> 00:27:13.690
You describe NeXTSTEP as an
environment and an operating

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:14.190
system.

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:16.317
And what I hear you talk
about is a great way

00:27:16.317 --> 00:27:17.400
to develop an application.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:18.608
I don't develop applications.

00:27:18.608 --> 00:27:20.688
I use other operating systems.

00:27:20.688 --> 00:27:25.560
Do you see a fracture of
the operating system market

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.960
into a specialized niche
that you're describing,

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.457
and then the general
market for those of us

00:27:30.457 --> 00:27:31.415
who don't develop apps?

00:27:31.415 --> 00:27:33.045
Or are you saying
that you can then

00:27:33.045 --> 00:27:35.461
bridge from an environment
that allows you to develop apps

00:27:35.461 --> 00:27:39.210
into an operating system that'll
be like Windows or MS DOS

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:40.590
on a lot of machines?

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:42.840
Well, let's get our
terminology straight.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:46.140
We look at NeXTSTEP as
an operating environment.

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.630
Much more than an
operating system.

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:53.050
Unix, which is our operating
system, is 10% of NeXTSTEP.

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:57.830
So in these modern
operating environments,

00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:00.440
when you develop a
custom app in NeXTSTEP,

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:02.840
it doesn't run on the
Mac or on Windows.

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:04.040
It can't.

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:06.770
You need all the objects
that come with NeXTSTEP

00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:08.122
to make it work.

00:28:08.122 --> 00:28:09.830
And let me go into a
little detail there.

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:12.680
How many of you
are technical here?

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:14.180
A lot, OK, great.

00:28:14.180 --> 00:28:16.890
Well, we've
discovered something.

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:20.100
You don't write code
any faster in NeXTSTEP

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:22.860
than you do in any other
operating and development

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:24.460
environment that we know of.

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:30.150
However, to do a particular app
on average, you write about 20%

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:34.710
of the code that you do in any
other development environment

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:35.490
we know of.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:40.200
About 20% of the code than
in Sun or in anything else.

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:44.100
So the code that's
the fastest to write,

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:46.680
the code that's the
easiest to maintain,

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:49.860
and the code that never breaks,
is the code you don't write.

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:53.410
So that's our strategy,
write a lot less code.

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:58.260
And the way we do this is
we enable the developer

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:01.620
to use a lot of objects
that others have written.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:04.860
We ship six years worth
of objects with NeXTSTEP.

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:07.719
You can create your own
objects for your own company,

00:29:07.719 --> 00:29:09.510
and then reuse them
around your developers.

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:12.060
And there's now independent
third party companies

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:15.150
not selling system software,
not selling applications,

00:29:15.150 --> 00:29:16.650
but selling objects.

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:20.490
There's not quite 10 of
them, and it's starting.

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:22.900
And I think it's going
to be a very big thing.

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:26.190
So in order to deliver
these apps that

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:28.450
are created on the NeXTSTEP,
you have to have NeXTSTEP

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:30.158
so they can run on
top and take advantage

00:29:30.158 --> 00:29:33.130
of this rich
community of objects.

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:35.604
And that's why we're
porting NeXTSTEP to the 486.

00:29:35.604 --> 00:29:38.020
That's why you'll see NeXTSTEP
running on several hardware

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:39.840
platforms.

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:45.230
And will there be a
fracturing, if you will?

00:29:45.230 --> 00:29:47.200
Sure, to some extent.

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:50.510
And I tend to look at
it as a transition.

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:52.790
Was there a fracturing
when Mac came out?

00:29:52.790 --> 00:29:54.060
Yes.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:56.300
And there's been
more of a transition,

00:29:56.300 --> 00:30:00.920
as people either move to Mac
or in the case of Windows,

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:04.040
adopt what's good about the Mac.

00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:05.930
Same thing's going
to happen here.

00:30:05.930 --> 00:30:07.850
We believe very strongly
that the benefits

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:10.280
from these object-oriented
environments-- not only just

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:12.980
rapid development, but a
much richer user environment.

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:15.860
If you use a NeXT, it's a
lot nicer than a Mac or a PC,

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:18.720
even if you never
developed an app.

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:23.190
Other environments will absorb
some of those breakthroughs,

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:25.830
and more and more people
will use NeXTSTEP.

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:29.160
And things will tend to balance
themselves out over time.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:31.140
But there will be a transition.

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:35.140
And our goal is to make
sure we're part of it.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:37.084
Yeah.

00:30:37.084 --> 00:30:39.514
You spoke earlier that
your competitive advantage,

00:30:39.514 --> 00:30:43.252
you felt lied in the fact that
these companies today were

00:30:43.252 --> 00:30:47.488
needing development on
the operational basis,

00:30:47.488 --> 00:30:50.362
as opposed to the management.

00:30:50.362 --> 00:30:52.278
Many companies, though--
or some, perhaps--

00:30:52.278 --> 00:30:53.444
outsource their development.

00:30:53.444 --> 00:30:56.590
How does this change
you business strategy?

00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:57.940
Good question.

00:30:57.940 --> 00:31:01.930
If our business strategy says
that we do a lot of things

00:31:01.930 --> 00:31:05.020
well, but the tip of our
arrow, or our Trojan horse,

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:08.530
which is getting us into these
large and medium-sized accounts

00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:11.500
is our custom application
development ability,

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:15.610
then our growth is going to
be paced among other things

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:19.660
by the development community
available to these companies

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:22.030
to develop these apps.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:24.820
Now, even though we have
shrunk the development time

00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:27.850
down to a fraction
of what it was, still

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:31.150
without developers out there,
we're not going to win.

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:37.900
Fortunately, most
companies have really

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:41.544
increased the staffs of good
people in their IS departments

00:31:41.544 --> 00:31:43.210
dramatically in the
last several years--

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:44.500
last four or five years.

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:46.630
There's now really
bright people who

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:49.720
know something about computer
science in IS departments.

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:51.994
And most of the
industries we talk to,

00:31:51.994 --> 00:31:54.160
whether they're healthcare,
financial services, even

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:57.940
law enforcement, places like
that have on-site developers

00:31:57.940 --> 00:31:58.880
in their IS teams.

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:01.990
If not, they're starting
to become a lot of vars,

00:32:01.990 --> 00:32:03.927
and people out there
that we're using

00:32:03.927 --> 00:32:06.010
in LA or in New York where
I just was, we probably

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:08.920
have about 10 independent third
party companies helping parts

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:10.670
of Wall Street develop things.

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:14.920
So I think that's not going
to be a stumbling block.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:16.300
My personal opinion
is the number

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:18.820
of people in applications
development is shrinking.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:21.400
It exploded in
the '80s with PCs,

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.750
but now that industry
is consolidating.

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:26.740
It's consolidating down
to very few companies.

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:28.660
And there's a lot
of excess people

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:31.630
out there, that I think are
starting to get channeled

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:35.160
into some of these other areas.

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:36.919
So far, it hasn't
been a problem.

00:32:36.919 --> 00:32:39.210
But if you want to do that,
please call us and tell us,

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:40.387
because we always need more.

00:32:40.387 --> 00:32:42.970
So what are you guys all going
to do when you get out of here?

00:32:42.970 --> 00:32:45.670
You going to go back
to your companies?

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:48.320
How many of you are
going to go back?

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:50.680
How many aren't
going to go back?

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:52.160
What are you going to do?

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:53.585
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:53.585 --> 00:32:55.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:57.756 --> 00:32:59.130
Well yeah, I think
we always have

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:01.692
jobs for really smart,
technical people.

00:33:01.692 --> 00:33:03.150
You know,
technically-based people.

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:06.180
And you guys are getting a
great business education here.

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:10.090
Yeah, we'd love to
talk to you about it.

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:10.970
Yes, sir.

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:13.190
Well, I don't want to
step on any toes here.

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:13.690
Go ahead.

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:15.814
But where do you Apple would
be had you not left it?

00:33:15.814 --> 00:33:18.105
And specifically, could it
have come out with something

00:33:18.105 --> 00:33:21.140
as revolutionary in
the tech industry?

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:22.550
Well, these are deep questions.

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:24.430
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:30.912
I'll tell you, I've obviously
thought about this a lot.

00:33:30.912 --> 00:33:32.620
And I don't want to
get into it too much.

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:38.700
But I will say that I
think everybody lost.

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:39.680
I think I lost.

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:41.760
And I wanted to
spend my life there.

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:43.590
I think Apple lost.

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:46.610
I think customers lost.

00:33:46.610 --> 00:33:50.380
And having said
all that, so what?

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:51.130
You go on.

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:53.410
It's not as bad as
a lot of things.

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:57.370
Not as bad as losing your arm.

00:33:57.370 --> 00:34:01.270
So people go on,
and companies go on.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:02.250
And I think Apple--

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:04.735
I'm very happy every
time Apple ships a Mac.

00:34:04.735 --> 00:34:06.760
It makes me very, very happy.

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:09.219
I think the PowerBooks
are decent products.

00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:11.469
I like them.

00:34:11.469 --> 00:34:13.522
But Apple has been struggling
the last few years.

00:34:13.522 --> 00:34:14.980
They've been having
a real struggle

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:16.920
with who they want to be.

00:34:16.920 --> 00:34:18.230
And this is nothing new.

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:19.600
We always had that.

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:22.770
That was part of what
kept Apple alive, I think.

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:24.400
And there were two
camps within Apple.

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:28.690
Camp one wanted to be the
next serious computer company,

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:33.280
and camp two wanted to sort
of be the Sony of computers.

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:37.870
And that struggling I think was
somewhat tearing Apple apart.

00:34:37.870 --> 00:34:41.020
And fortunately, the
Sony guys have won.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:43.576
They've kind of decided to
go be the Sony of computers.

00:34:43.576 --> 00:34:45.159
And so the PowerBooks
are pretty good,

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:48.610
but the Quadras suck wind
right now, the high end stuff.

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:54.219
And they're basically not
putting a lot of resources

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:57.010
onto the power
users on desktops,

00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:59.080
and they've put most
of their best people

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:02.052
now on the portables
and on consumer products

00:35:02.052 --> 00:35:03.760
that they're going to
be coming out with.

00:35:03.760 --> 00:35:05.710
And I think they'll
do very well at that.

00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:10.260
Now, there's a problem
in there, in that

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:12.630
if you look at the
consumer products that

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:17.835
sell over a million a year, you
can count them on a few hands.

00:35:17.835 --> 00:35:18.960
It turns out, there's not--

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:20.250
I mean, consumer
electronics products,

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:21.166
not like toothbrushes.

00:35:21.166 --> 00:35:22.230
But electronics products.

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:26.642
And so let's assume they have
one of those or two of those.

00:35:26.642 --> 00:35:29.100
Let's say they have a product
that sells two million a year

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:31.200
at a $500 ASP to them.

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:36.060
So it sells to the consumer at
$795 or something like that.

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:39.230
That's what, a billion
dollars, right?

00:35:39.230 --> 00:35:41.510
two million a year, $500 apiece.

00:35:41.510 --> 00:35:42.770
It's a billion dollars.

00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:45.110
They still have to get the
other $7 billion of revenue

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:47.700
somewhere.

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:51.070
So it's going to be an
interesting transition

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:53.320
as the ASPs get lower.

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:55.520
The volumes have to
get much, much higher,

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:58.169
and you run into
some scale issues

00:35:58.169 --> 00:36:00.460
when you look out at the
consumer electronics industry.

00:36:00.460 --> 00:36:02.015
Not that it's not possible.

00:36:02.015 --> 00:36:04.390
And I think it's going to be
really interesting to watch.

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:10.480
And we also see a lot of not
quite that clarity of movement,

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:14.980
but some movement in
the Windows and PC world

00:36:14.980 --> 00:36:17.980
again towards taking
what they have now

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:19.720
and making it portable.

00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:24.640
But not giving the top third
of the market more power.

00:36:24.640 --> 00:36:26.530
You know, System 7 on
the Mac was supposed

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:27.897
to be the second coming.

00:36:27.897 --> 00:36:28.480
And it wasn't.

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:31.090
It turned out to be an
incremental improvement.

00:36:31.090 --> 00:36:33.289
OS/2 was supposed to take
us into new dimensions,

00:36:33.289 --> 00:36:35.080
and it turned out to
be a complete failure.

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:37.038
And they're going to
throw another $100 million

00:36:37.038 --> 00:36:39.705
after it just to make sure
you all know about it.

00:36:39.705 --> 00:36:40.660
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:45.100
And so there is a
third of those desktops

00:36:45.100 --> 00:36:50.530
out there that the users and the
people that want to deploy more

00:36:50.530 --> 00:36:52.720
sophisticated apps are--

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:55.600
they're in the desert
wanting something to drink.

00:36:55.600 --> 00:36:57.580
And I think there's a
tremendous opportunity

00:36:57.580 --> 00:36:59.060
to give them some solutions.

00:36:59.060 --> 00:37:00.760
Now, that doesn't
mean that Apple is not

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:01.285
going to be successful.

00:37:01.285 --> 00:37:03.743
It just means they're going to
go off in another direction.

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:08.040
Who knows what
would have happened

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:11.170
had all this not happened.

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:14.032
Yes, up in the corner?

00:37:14.032 --> 00:37:15.820
You've talked a lot
about making products.

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:18.366
I wonder if you could talk
about the management at NeXT,

00:37:18.366 --> 00:37:22.400
and if you see changing
at all, as you hope, go

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:25.795
from $120 million company
to a $1 billion company.

00:37:25.795 --> 00:37:26.295
Sure.

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:33.650
Yeah, we're-- we've done
a lot in the last year.

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:35.720
The most significant
things we did

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:38.900
were, one, we hired
this guy right here.

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:41.550
Mike Slade is our
VP of Marketing.

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:43.868
He spent-- you can stand up.

00:37:43.868 --> 00:37:44.820
[INAUDIBLE]

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:45.320
No.

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:48.224
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:54.720
And he's cheap, too.

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:57.130
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:57.130 --> 00:38:00.340
And Mike spent seven or
eight years at Microsoft.

00:38:00.340 --> 00:38:03.941
And I originally met him, he was
the Product Manager for Excel

00:38:03.941 --> 00:38:05.440
when it first came
out, and ended up

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:07.420
running big pieces of
marketing for Microsoft.

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:09.044
So Mike runs all the
marketing at NeXT.

00:38:09.044 --> 00:38:12.114
And Mike came in to
NeXT just about the time

00:38:12.114 --> 00:38:14.530
when we were finally hearing
what these big companies were

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:16.450
saying about mission
critical custom apps.

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:18.850
And so we've worked
very closely together

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:21.080
to redefine the marketing
strategy of the company.

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:24.010
And Mike is done just
a superb job at that.

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:29.824
The second big thing we did
was, about three months ago,

00:38:29.824 --> 00:38:31.240
a little over three
months ago, we

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:36.850
consolidated our hardware design
engineering, our manufacturing,

00:38:36.850 --> 00:38:39.070
our worldwide distribution,
and hardware service

00:38:39.070 --> 00:38:42.160
all into one chunk called
the hardware division.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:45.035
So from cradle to grave, they
have hardware responsibility.

00:38:45.035 --> 00:38:47.410
And we're in the process of
forming the software division

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:53.100
right now, so that we're all
still under the same roof,

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:55.080
but we have clarity
about the fact

00:38:55.080 --> 00:39:00.060
that we've got to make
two businesses successful.

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:02.160
The third thing
that we did was--

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:03.720
and we have a
really great person

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:05.735
running that, Rich Page, who
is one of the founders of NeXT.

00:39:05.735 --> 00:39:07.401
And he's doing a great
job running that.

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:13.630
We hired a new CFO
recently, Marcel Gani.

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:17.472
He's from Intel, spent
12 years at Intel doing

00:39:17.472 --> 00:39:18.680
some pretty interesting jobs.

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:20.579
He ran all of
finance for Europe.

00:39:20.579 --> 00:39:22.370
He ran all of internal
audit for the Board.

00:39:22.370 --> 00:39:24.734
He ran all of manufacturing,
planning, and scheduling

00:39:24.734 --> 00:39:26.150
for a while, and
things like that.

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:30.000
He's a pretty good guy, went to
Cyprus for a year and a half,

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:30.980
and then came to NeXT.

00:39:30.980 --> 00:39:32.750
And he's great.

00:39:32.750 --> 00:39:34.970
And then recently,
as a matter of fact,

00:39:34.970 --> 00:39:39.860
about last week, I hired a
Chief Operating Officer, a guy

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:41.330
named Peter van Cuylenburg.

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:44.952
And Peter is someone
I first met when

00:39:44.952 --> 00:39:46.910
we were looking for an
Executive Vice President

00:39:46.910 --> 00:39:48.134
to the Sales and Marketing.

00:39:48.134 --> 00:39:49.550
And he turned me
down for the job.

00:39:49.550 --> 00:39:52.460
And we subsequently decided
not to hire that position.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:55.350
And I've been chasing him
for the last year and a half

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:55.850
almost.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:57.600
He's really good.

00:39:57.600 --> 00:40:02.480
He spent a lot of time at
TI as [? run ?] Europe,

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:05.630
and about three years ago, went
to a company called Mercury

00:40:05.630 --> 00:40:10.370
Communications in England--
they're the MCI of the UK--

00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:13.250
and turned that company
around to about $2 billion,

00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:16.470
and then was promoted
to run half of Cable &amp;--

00:40:16.470 --> 00:40:19.025
or half of Mercury's parent
company, Cable &amp; Wireless,

00:40:19.025 --> 00:40:20.230
and was running--

00:40:20.230 --> 00:40:22.610
they're about $6 billion.

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:25.659
And he went there when he
thought that communications

00:40:25.659 --> 00:40:27.200
and computing were
going to, kind of,

00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:29.960
come together, but
never happened.

00:40:29.960 --> 00:40:31.904
And he figured out
that his real love

00:40:31.904 --> 00:40:33.070
is in the computer business.

00:40:33.070 --> 00:40:35.350
He's very, very
good, operationally,

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:37.107
so that's just
happened last week.

00:40:37.107 --> 00:40:39.440
It seems like all the good
people I really want to hire,

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:40.981
it seems take me a
year to hire them.

00:40:40.981 --> 00:40:43.670
Them And it's always been
that way, even at Apple.

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:45.775
Some of the best technical
people, or whoever,

00:40:45.775 --> 00:40:48.150
it always seemed to take me,
like, a year to pry them out

00:40:48.150 --> 00:40:53.560
of HP, or wherever, and took
me over a year to hire--

00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:54.740
I think Mike has the award.

00:40:54.740 --> 00:40:58.100
You're about a year and a half.

00:40:58.100 --> 00:41:00.050
And they're all worth it.

00:41:00.050 --> 00:41:03.200
What happens is, I usually meet
somebody that is really good--

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:04.730
I think is very, very good.

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:05.930
And you can't get them.

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:07.910
And then you go try
to find other people.

00:41:07.910 --> 00:41:09.029
And nobody measures up.

00:41:09.029 --> 00:41:10.820
You know, when you meet
somebody that good,

00:41:10.820 --> 00:41:12.861
just, you always compare
them to this one person.

00:41:12.861 --> 00:41:15.200
And you know you're going to
be settling for second best

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:15.949
if you compromise.

00:41:15.949 --> 00:41:18.440
And I've always
found it best not

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:21.950
to compromise, and just
keep chipping away.

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:26.966
So I think we're
doing quite a bit.

00:41:26.966 --> 00:41:28.840
It feels like we're
running the company a lot

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:30.850
better now than we ever have.

00:41:30.850 --> 00:41:34.372
We've definitely made
our share of mistakes.

00:41:34.372 --> 00:41:35.368
Yeah?

00:41:35.368 --> 00:41:37.692
What technological
advances do you

00:41:37.692 --> 00:41:40.348
see coming over the
next five to 10 years?

00:41:40.348 --> 00:41:46.011
and how is NeXTSTEP structured
to take advantage of those?

00:41:46.011 --> 00:41:47.260
I'll give you a global answer.

00:41:47.260 --> 00:41:48.843
Then we can descend
into some details.

00:41:52.870 --> 00:41:57.790
I believe that you can use
the concept of technology

00:41:57.790 --> 00:42:03.184
windows opening, and
then eventually closing.

00:42:03.184 --> 00:42:08.000
And what I mean by that
is, enough technology,

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:10.250
usually from fairly
diverse places,

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:14.840
comes together, and makes
something that's a quantum

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:16.262
leap forward possible.

00:42:16.262 --> 00:42:17.720
And it doesn't come
out of nowhere.

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:21.020
If you poke around the labs, and
you hang around the Media Lab

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:23.900
here at MIT and other places,
you can, kind of, get a feel

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:26.250
for some of those things.

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:28.180
And usually, they're
not quite possible.

00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:30.630
But, all of a sudden, you
start to sense things coming

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:32.250
together, and the
planets lining up,

00:42:32.250 --> 00:42:35.880
to where this is now
possible, or barely possible.

00:42:35.880 --> 00:42:38.900
And a window opens up.

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:41.476
And it usually takes around--

00:42:41.476 --> 00:42:43.100
my experience anyway,
my life has been,

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:49.640
it takes around five years to
create a commercial product

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:54.170
that takes advantage of that
technical window opening up.

00:42:54.170 --> 00:42:56.450
Sometimes you start before
the window is quite open.

00:42:56.450 --> 00:42:57.400
And you can't get through it.

00:42:57.400 --> 00:42:58.191
And you push it up.

00:42:58.191 --> 00:42:59.090
And you push it up.

00:42:59.090 --> 00:43:01.010
Sometimes it just
takes a lot of work,

00:43:01.010 --> 00:43:03.440
took that long
with the Apple II,

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:05.690
took that long with the Mac.

00:43:05.690 --> 00:43:09.030
You know, it took a Lisa
along the way, $100 million.

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:10.100
It takes a while.

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:13.850
It's a expensive to
push those windows open.

00:43:13.850 --> 00:43:17.270
And in our case, you know,
our first product failed.

00:43:17.270 --> 00:43:18.800
We came out with this cube.

00:43:18.800 --> 00:43:20.780
And we sold 10,000 of them.

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:21.380
Why?

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:23.344
Because we weren't
quite there yet.

00:43:23.344 --> 00:43:25.010
And we made some
mistakes along the way.

00:43:25.010 --> 00:43:26.540
And we had to course correct.

00:43:26.540 --> 00:43:29.600
You know, Macintosh was a
course correction off the Lisa.

00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:33.140
So with Apple II and III,
we did it in reverse.

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:41.620
It takes around five years, or
some number of years like that,

00:43:41.620 --> 00:43:44.060
to realize that window opening.

00:43:44.060 --> 00:43:46.310
And then it seems to take
about another five years

00:43:46.310 --> 00:43:49.190
to really exploit it
in the marketplace.

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:51.380
And let me give
you some examples.

00:43:51.380 --> 00:43:52.100
from my life.

00:43:52.100 --> 00:43:58.185
Apple II lasted 15
years, 15 years.

00:43:58.185 --> 00:43:59.060
The hardware churned.

00:43:59.060 --> 00:44:01.220
But basically, it was
the same for 15 years.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:05.140
DOS-- you know, DOS,
just passed 10 years.

00:44:05.140 --> 00:44:06.780
I don't think anyone
would disagree

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:10.090
that it's going to easily
last another five, right--

00:44:10.090 --> 00:44:12.830
unfortunately.

00:44:12.830 --> 00:44:16.695
And Mac, you know, Mac is
eight years old, right?

00:44:16.695 --> 00:44:18.820
No question it's going to
last another four or five

00:44:18.820 --> 00:44:19.930
years, right?

00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:21.760
These things are hard.

00:44:21.760 --> 00:44:23.620
They don't last because
it's convenient,

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:25.564
or even because it's economic.

00:44:25.564 --> 00:44:26.980
They last because
they're really--

00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:29.410
this is hard stuff to do.

00:44:29.410 --> 00:44:35.321
And so when we are
pushing that window open,

00:44:35.321 --> 00:44:37.320
I think with our current
generation of products,

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:38.940
we finally got the window open.

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:40.530
After six years, it's open.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:43.440
We've got an extremely
elegant implementation.

00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:46.230
And we've got five years
of work to do to exploit it

00:44:46.230 --> 00:44:48.000
in the marketplace.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:49.860
You know, we'll
peak in five years.

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:51.235
Five years, we'll
all sit around,

00:44:51.235 --> 00:44:53.840
and say, OK, it's time to get
started on the next thing.

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:55.840
It's time to get going
on the next thing-- maybe

00:44:55.840 --> 00:44:57.010
four years from now.

00:44:57.010 --> 00:44:58.840
But we've got a lot
of work ahead of us

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:01.870
just to move this thing
out, and educate the market,

00:45:01.870 --> 00:45:04.840
and continue to refine it
based on market feedback.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:09.490
So everything I know about
technology windows that

00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:12.400
are open, or just about
open, is in NeXTSTEP,

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:14.674
or we're working
on it in the labs.

00:45:14.674 --> 00:45:17.090
And these things generally
don't come along independently.

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:18.965
They, kind of-- clumps
of them come together,

00:45:18.965 --> 00:45:20.500
has been my experience.

00:45:20.500 --> 00:45:24.780
So the things that aren't
in there right now that I

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:27.822
can talk about, there's
some video stuff that's

00:45:27.822 --> 00:45:30.030
really interesting, that's
going to be integrated in.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:32.730
There some security stuff
that's really fascinating

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:33.990
that's integrating in.

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:37.380
But most of the core
technologies in there--

00:45:37.380 --> 00:45:39.180
products are getting
smaller and portable.

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:40.960
Products are getting
much, much faster.

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:42.990
But these things are well known.

00:45:42.990 --> 00:45:45.690
You know, the products
that we can put on--

00:45:45.690 --> 00:45:48.300
that we can give to you
in the next year or two

00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:50.760
are going to be
running at speeds

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:53.010
that I find hard to believe.

00:45:53.010 --> 00:45:55.380
And I've been doing
this for a while.

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:58.120
So I think the windows open.

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:00.820
And I think
object-oriented technology

00:46:00.820 --> 00:46:04.550
is the biggest
technical breakthrough

00:46:04.550 --> 00:46:07.480
I have seen since
the early '80s,

00:46:07.480 --> 00:46:09.844
with the graphical
user interfaces.

00:46:09.844 --> 00:46:11.260
And I think it's
bigger, actually.

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:13.830
It will prove to be
bigger over time.

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:14.608
Yes?

00:46:14.608 --> 00:46:15.564
I have a question.

00:46:15.564 --> 00:46:18.193
If the value
[INAUDIBLE] and things

00:46:18.193 --> 00:46:21.300
like operational
applications, and NeXTSTEP

00:46:21.300 --> 00:46:23.690
is a tool to help
you build those,

00:46:23.690 --> 00:46:25.690
where do you draw the
line in capturing the most

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:27.910
value between actually
developing applications,

00:46:27.910 --> 00:46:30.312
something like Word or
Excel, and becoming a tool

00:46:30.312 --> 00:46:32.812
builder to support those people
who may capture a little bit

00:46:32.812 --> 00:46:34.210
more of the value?

00:46:34.210 --> 00:46:37.082
Where do you want to
draw the line there?

00:46:37.082 --> 00:46:38.290
If you're a software company?

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:40.130
If you're a software company.

00:46:40.130 --> 00:46:42.097
Well, Microsoft
has made it easy.

00:46:42.097 --> 00:46:42.930
Repeat the question.

00:46:42.930 --> 00:46:44.500
Yeah, oh, sorry,
the question is,

00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:46.750
if you're a software company,
should you develop apps,

00:46:46.750 --> 00:46:48.870
or should you develop
objects and tools?

00:46:48.870 --> 00:46:51.520
And see, the software
industry, with Microsoft,

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:53.407
and Lotus, and WordPerfect
made it real easy.

00:46:53.407 --> 00:46:55.240
Unless you've got $20
million or $30 million

00:46:55.240 --> 00:46:57.790
burning a hole in your pocket
so you can go out and hire

00:46:57.790 --> 00:47:00.670
a few hundred people to develop
what it takes to develop one

00:47:00.670 --> 00:47:05.050
of these polished shrink-wrapped
apps on Mac or on Windows,

00:47:05.050 --> 00:47:07.900
then you don't have
much of a choice.

00:47:07.900 --> 00:47:09.820
There's not a real
opportunity there to--

00:47:09.820 --> 00:47:12.580
assuming you had the
best spreadsheet today,

00:47:12.580 --> 00:47:14.500
you could easily
spend $50 million

00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:18.400
marketing it before you'd
be breaking even selling it,

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:22.420
because of just how expensive
it is to market a product today.

00:47:22.420 --> 00:47:24.526
But that's only for
existing spreadsheets.

00:47:24.526 --> 00:47:26.942
If you're trying to develop a
next-level generation of it,

00:47:26.942 --> 00:47:28.690
a different type of product.

00:47:28.690 --> 00:47:33.190
Well, assume that you have
a breakthrough spreadsheet.

00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:39.030
Again, on mainstream platforms,
it will take $50 million

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:42.570
to just rise above the
noise level and market it.

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:46.680
So what the brightest people
I know of today are doing

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:48.360
is they're writing objects.

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:51.690
They're writing hunks of
things that other developers

00:47:51.690 --> 00:47:55.050
are going to use to build apps.

00:47:55.050 --> 00:47:58.740
And they're going
where everybody isn't.

00:47:58.740 --> 00:48:02.570
And that's, I think, going
to be the next new thing.

00:48:02.570 --> 00:48:03.071
Yeah?

00:48:03.071 --> 00:48:04.986
You mentioned that a
possible problem for Macs

00:48:04.986 --> 00:48:07.123
is the lack of developers
and consultants out there

00:48:07.123 --> 00:48:08.482
who recommend it.

00:48:08.482 --> 00:48:12.100
Based on that view,
what's the NeXT view

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:14.247
of how to market itself
to academic institutions?

00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:20.250
I think your
question is, what is

00:48:20.250 --> 00:48:22.860
our philosophy on marketing our
products to higher education?

00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:25.180
Mhm.

00:48:25.180 --> 00:48:26.610
Well, we started
off selling only

00:48:26.610 --> 00:48:30.570
to higher education, which
arguably, was a mistake.

00:48:30.570 --> 00:48:32.739
And But we've done
really well there.

00:48:32.739 --> 00:48:33.780
And our hearts are there.

00:48:33.780 --> 00:48:35.970
And we sold a lot of
Macintoshes there too,

00:48:35.970 --> 00:48:37.230
when we were at Apple.

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:40.409
And I think it's a no-brainer.

00:48:40.409 --> 00:48:41.700
I think you take your products.

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:43.366
You discount them as
heavily as you can.

00:48:43.366 --> 00:48:45.000
You sell them into
higher education.

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:46.920
Higher education is
a wonderful place

00:48:46.920 --> 00:48:48.600
to give you great
feedback about how

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:51.250
to make your products better,
and what's wrong with them.

00:48:51.250 --> 00:48:55.774
And it's a great place
to educate bright people

00:48:55.774 --> 00:48:57.690
that you can hire, and
that your customers can

00:48:57.690 --> 00:49:00.050
hire when they graduate,
so we do exactly that.

00:49:00.050 --> 00:49:03.420
We have-- I think were
sold on about 350 campuses

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:04.740
in the United States.

00:49:04.740 --> 00:49:07.380
We are clearly the number
one selling workstation

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:08.700
across the US.

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:11.730
And we're the number two
selling computer of any type

00:49:11.730 --> 00:49:14.550
at campuses, like
here MIT or Stanford,

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:15.870
right behind the Macintosh.

00:49:15.870 --> 00:49:19.860
We sell more computers
at MIT than PCs

00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:24.810
through the institutional
resale engines.

00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:27.400
So we do as much as we can.

00:49:27.400 --> 00:49:29.290
And I think it's been
pretty effective.

00:49:29.290 --> 00:49:30.251
What do you think?

00:49:30.251 --> 00:49:32.556
I mean, for example,
[INAUDIBLE] is going away.

00:49:32.556 --> 00:49:34.560
Like, the support
[INAUDIBLE] is going.

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:35.060
Right.

00:49:35.060 --> 00:49:37.016
I mean, I'm talking about,
not just selling computers.

00:49:37.016 --> 00:49:38.972
I'm talking about
establishing a network

00:49:38.972 --> 00:49:43.380
of institutionalized
system [INAUDIBLE]..

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:45.620
Well, what's happening is--

00:49:45.620 --> 00:49:49.420
see, the Project Athenas
didn't succeed for a reason.

00:49:49.420 --> 00:49:52.800
They didn't succeed, because
they had a lot of good ideas

00:49:52.800 --> 00:49:55.710
as a research project,
but the people

00:49:55.710 --> 00:49:57.720
necessary to
commercialize those things

00:49:57.720 --> 00:50:00.510
and make them into
real products aren't

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:02.400
necessarily the
same people that are

00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:06.070
going to pioneer the ideas
at a university like MIT.

00:50:06.070 --> 00:50:10.140
And so those projects
never quite get baked.

00:50:10.140 --> 00:50:11.670
The recipes, kind of, developed.

00:50:11.670 --> 00:50:13.090
And you make a few samples.

00:50:13.090 --> 00:50:15.210
And it's pretty good,
but the computer industry

00:50:15.210 --> 00:50:16.214
is pretty advanced.

00:50:16.214 --> 00:50:17.880
And so other people
pick up those ideas,

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:19.020
and make them into
real products.

00:50:19.020 --> 00:50:21.436
And they, kind of, leave the
research projects in the dust

00:50:21.436 --> 00:50:24.120
if the researchers
drop research and start

00:50:24.120 --> 00:50:25.470
trying to commercialize stuff.

00:50:25.470 --> 00:50:28.930
And I can point to 100 examples
of that in higher education.

00:50:28.930 --> 00:50:31.800
So it's probably good
that Project Athena has

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:33.720
a beginning, a
middle, and an end,

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:35.400
so that those people
don't get stuck

00:50:35.400 --> 00:50:38.310
trying to do commercial software
in an academic environment.

00:50:38.310 --> 00:50:41.250
It's, kind of, a mismatch.

00:50:41.250 --> 00:50:44.041
As far as we're concerned,
what we're doing is,

00:50:44.041 --> 00:50:45.540
there's a lot of
labs that are being

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:47.307
put in in higher education.

00:50:47.307 --> 00:50:49.140
And we're winning almost
every one of those.

00:50:49.140 --> 00:50:50.860
And we really go
hard after the lab,

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:52.776
so that people that can't
afford the computers

00:50:52.776 --> 00:50:54.450
have public access.

00:50:54.450 --> 00:50:58.310
And most universities now have
fairly elaborate campus-wide

00:50:58.310 --> 00:50:58.810
networks.

00:50:58.810 --> 00:51:01.102
It's no longer a new
cutting-edge thing.

00:51:01.102 --> 00:51:02.310
And we plug right into those.

00:51:02.310 --> 00:51:04.620
So I think it's--

00:51:04.620 --> 00:51:06.990
life's moved beyond where it
was a few years ago, where

00:51:06.990 --> 00:51:09.270
those kind of projects
were really important.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:13.260
And the knowledge to do that
exists fairly widespread.

00:51:13.260 --> 00:51:15.180
I think a few more and-- yeah?

00:51:15.180 --> 00:51:17.575
What's the most important
thing that you personally

00:51:17.575 --> 00:51:20.900
learned at Apple, that
you're doing at NeXT?

00:51:20.900 --> 00:51:21.730
Good question.

00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:44.890
I'm not sure I learned
this when I was at Apple,

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:50.770
but I learned it based on
the data when I was at Apple.

00:51:50.770 --> 00:52:00.610
And that is, I now take a
longer-term view on people.

00:52:00.610 --> 00:52:04.420
In other words, when I see
something not being done right,

00:52:04.420 --> 00:52:06.440
my first reaction
isn't to go fix it.

00:52:09.160 --> 00:52:13.814
It's to say, we're
building a team here.

00:52:13.814 --> 00:52:16.230
And we're going to do great
stuff for the next decade, not

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:19.710
just the next year,
and so what do

00:52:19.710 --> 00:52:24.000
I need to do to help so that
the person that's screwing up

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:28.240
learns versus how do
I fix the problem?

00:52:28.240 --> 00:52:32.260
And that's painful sometimes.

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:35.320
And I still have that first
instinct to go fix the problem.

00:52:35.320 --> 00:52:38.470
But that's taking
a longer-term view

00:52:38.470 --> 00:52:40.930
and people is probably the
biggest thing that's changed.

00:52:40.930 --> 00:52:42.013
And then and I don't know.

00:52:42.013 --> 00:52:44.983
That's maybe the part
that's biological, but--

00:52:44.983 --> 00:52:45.965
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:48.145
Yes?

00:52:48.145 --> 00:52:49.824
To pull off on that
question, I wanted

00:52:49.824 --> 00:52:51.449
to ask you about your
management style,

00:52:51.449 --> 00:52:53.894
and specifically, how
do you resolve conflict

00:52:53.894 --> 00:52:55.361
in your organization?

00:52:56.184 --> 00:52:57.350
What's our management style?

00:52:57.350 --> 00:53:00.370
How do we resolve conflict?

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:02.200
I've never believed
in the theory

00:53:02.200 --> 00:53:04.690
that, if we're on the
same management team,

00:53:04.690 --> 00:53:08.140
and a decision has to be
made, and I decide in a way

00:53:08.140 --> 00:53:12.300
that you don't like, and I say,
come on, buy into the decision.

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:13.276
You know, buy into it.

00:53:13.276 --> 00:53:14.650
Like, we're all
in the same team,

00:53:14.650 --> 00:53:16.210
you don't agree,
but buy into it.

00:53:16.210 --> 00:53:17.800
Let's go make it happen.

00:53:17.800 --> 00:53:22.120
Because what happens is,
sooner or later, you're

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:25.990
paying somebody to do
what they think is right,

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:28.090
but then you're trying
to get them to do

00:53:28.090 --> 00:53:30.040
what they think isn't right.

00:53:30.040 --> 00:53:31.630
And sooner or later, it outs.

00:53:31.630 --> 00:53:34.040
And you end up
having that conflict.

00:53:34.040 --> 00:53:37.540
So I've always
felt that the best

00:53:37.540 --> 00:53:39.670
way is to get
everybody in a room,

00:53:39.670 --> 00:53:43.090
and talk it through
until you agree.

00:53:43.090 --> 00:53:46.060
Now, that's not
everybody in the company,

00:53:46.060 --> 00:53:49.840
but that's everybody
that's really

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:53.920
involved in that decision,
that needs to execute it.

00:53:53.920 --> 00:53:58.450
And so that's how
we try to run NeXT.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:00.640
The way we run NeXT is,
we have a team at the top

00:54:00.640 --> 00:54:01.730
we call the Policy Team.

00:54:01.730 --> 00:54:04.660
There's eight people.

00:54:04.660 --> 00:54:05.470
Mike is on it.

00:54:05.470 --> 00:54:05.980
I'm on it.

00:54:05.980 --> 00:54:07.550
We have six other people on it.

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:10.550
And the key-- we have
two things we try to do.

00:54:10.550 --> 00:54:13.600
One is, we try to differentiate
between the really

00:54:13.600 --> 00:54:16.690
important decisions and the
ones that we don't have to make.

00:54:16.690 --> 00:54:19.090
And the really
important ones, we

00:54:19.090 --> 00:54:23.650
work on it until we all agree,
because we're paying people

00:54:23.650 --> 00:54:25.270
to tell us what to do.

00:54:25.270 --> 00:54:26.770
In other words, I
don't view that we

00:54:26.770 --> 00:54:28.420
pay people to do things.

00:54:28.420 --> 00:54:30.310
That's easy, to find
people to do things.

00:54:30.310 --> 00:54:32.590
What's harder is to
find people to tell you

00:54:32.590 --> 00:54:34.140
what should be done, right?

00:54:34.140 --> 00:54:35.140
That's what we look for.

00:54:35.140 --> 00:54:37.960
So we pay people a lot
of money, and we expect

00:54:37.960 --> 00:54:39.640
them to tell us what to do.

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:42.959
And so when that's
your attitude,

00:54:42.959 --> 00:54:45.250
you shouldn't run off and do
things if people don't all

00:54:45.250 --> 00:54:47.362
feel good about them.

00:54:47.362 --> 00:54:49.570
And the key to making that
work is to realize there's

00:54:49.570 --> 00:54:53.430
not that many things that any
one team really has to decide.

00:54:53.430 --> 00:54:57.490
And we might have 25
really important things

00:54:57.490 --> 00:55:02.210
we have to decide on
a year, not a lot.

00:55:02.210 --> 00:55:04.850
So that's how we try to run it.

00:55:04.850 --> 00:55:05.660
Sometimes it works.

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:09.270
And sometimes we're
still working on it.

00:55:09.270 --> 00:55:11.730
I can't think of once--

00:55:11.730 --> 00:55:13.440
I can't-- maybe
there's once or twice,

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:16.320
but I can't even recall a
time when I've said, dammit,

00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:17.160
I'm the CEO.

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:21.210
And we're doing it
this way, you know?

00:55:21.210 --> 00:55:24.450
I can recall a time when I've
said, we don't see eye-to-eye,

00:55:24.450 --> 00:55:25.744
and you're off the team.

00:55:25.744 --> 00:55:26.726
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:26.726 --> 00:55:27.226
You know?

00:55:31.180 --> 00:55:32.650
I've had to say
that once or twice,

00:55:32.650 --> 00:55:37.900
over a prolonged period of
time, when a person has not

00:55:37.900 --> 00:55:40.000
wanted to go in the
same direction we've

00:55:40.000 --> 00:55:41.350
wanted to go in as a team.

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:43.810
It's my job every once
in a while to say, hey,

00:55:43.810 --> 00:55:44.860
you want to go this way?

00:55:44.860 --> 00:55:46.420
We want to go this way.

00:55:46.420 --> 00:55:49.280
It's not working.

00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:53.916
But when people are on the
team, then we work it out.

00:55:56.580 --> 00:55:57.080
Yeah?

00:55:57.080 --> 00:55:59.072
You've, kind of,
chosen this direction,

00:55:59.072 --> 00:56:02.558
this niche towards
the premium product,

00:56:02.558 --> 00:56:04.550
versus you've
discussed Macintosh

00:56:04.550 --> 00:56:06.044
going into
portability, and a lot

00:56:06.044 --> 00:56:07.210
of these portability issues.

00:56:07.210 --> 00:56:10.028
Do you think portability
is exclusive?

00:56:10.028 --> 00:56:13.763
Can you do that and still do
some of the market-driven type

00:56:13.763 --> 00:56:15.869
stuff as well?

00:56:15.869 --> 00:56:17.660
I want to come back to
your premium product

00:56:17.660 --> 00:56:20.300
characterization, because
our products actually cost

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:22.550
close to half of what Sun's do.

00:56:22.550 --> 00:56:24.890
And the reason they do is,
we have the most automated

00:56:24.890 --> 00:56:26.310
factory in the industry.

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:28.124
And we have a great
VLSI design group,

00:56:28.124 --> 00:56:29.790
which designs stuff
in a lot less parts.

00:56:29.790 --> 00:56:31.640
Do you guys-- manufacturing,
do you care about that?

00:56:31.640 --> 00:56:31.730
Yeah.

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:32.165
Yes.

00:56:32.165 --> 00:56:32.665
Yes.

00:56:32.665 --> 00:56:33.470
Yeah.

00:56:33.470 --> 00:56:37.050
We should talk about
that in a minute.

00:56:37.050 --> 00:56:39.710
The industry is
bifurcating right now.

00:56:39.710 --> 00:56:42.290
And what's happening is, is
that the Macs and the PCs as you

00:56:42.290 --> 00:56:44.330
know them today are all going
to be just like this-- hold up

00:56:44.330 --> 00:56:45.280
your PowerBook.

00:56:45.280 --> 00:56:47.446
They're all going to be
just like this, only lighter

00:56:47.446 --> 00:56:50.300
and smaller, before very long.

00:56:50.300 --> 00:56:53.510
And they're taking the
technology we have today,

00:56:53.510 --> 00:56:56.000
not particularly changing
it, and getting it

00:56:56.000 --> 00:56:58.767
off the desktop
in portable forms.

00:56:58.767 --> 00:57:00.600
And they're giving up
a few things for that,

00:57:00.600 --> 00:57:04.760
but nothing terribly profound.

00:57:04.760 --> 00:57:07.170
However, we're getting
all sorts of signals

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:08.970
from certain parts
of the markets

00:57:08.970 --> 00:57:12.397
that they want things that
are the enemy of that, right?

00:57:12.397 --> 00:57:13.230
Well, what are they?

00:57:13.230 --> 00:57:17.640
Well, speed-- speed is
the enemy of portability,

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:20.550
because speed
takes power, right?

00:57:20.550 --> 00:57:23.240
So the kind of speed
that our customers want,

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:25.870
it would run for 3 and
1/2 minutes on batteries.

00:57:25.870 --> 00:57:28.512
And that's useless.

00:57:28.512 --> 00:57:29.970
The second thing
they want is, they

00:57:29.970 --> 00:57:31.844
want a lot more storage
on their disk drives,

00:57:31.844 --> 00:57:34.290
and a lot more memory, again,
the enemy of portability

00:57:34.290 --> 00:57:36.960
for power and size reasons.

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:40.030
Another thing they want is
really high-speed networking,

00:57:40.030 --> 00:57:40.530
right?

00:57:40.530 --> 00:57:43.410
The radio LANs on
these things, at best,

00:57:43.410 --> 00:57:45.850
are going to go 19.2
kilobits per second.

00:57:45.850 --> 00:57:48.777
Our customers want 100
megabits per second and higher.

00:57:48.777 --> 00:57:50.610
You're not going to do
that with a radio LAN

00:57:50.610 --> 00:57:52.029
anytime in the next five years.

00:57:52.029 --> 00:57:53.820
Another thing our
customers want is our mix

00:57:53.820 --> 00:57:55.920
has shifted to, like, 80% color.

00:57:55.920 --> 00:57:57.930
And they want true
color to do photographs.

00:57:57.930 --> 00:58:00.510
There is no flat panel
color display today

00:58:00.510 --> 00:58:02.490
that will do
photographic-quality color.

00:58:02.490 --> 00:58:05.330
And they want big ones, because
once you have multitasking,

00:58:05.330 --> 00:58:06.788
and you run a bunch
things at once,

00:58:06.788 --> 00:58:09.039
you need a bigger window
into this electronic world.

00:58:09.039 --> 00:58:11.580
When you're using your computer
for two or three hours a day,

00:58:11.580 --> 00:58:14.550
you don't want to be looking
through blinders this big.

00:58:14.550 --> 00:58:16.600
You want something bigger.

00:58:16.600 --> 00:58:20.550
So these are all the
enemy of that today.

00:58:20.550 --> 00:58:24.060
And we're working on
smaller products, for sure,

00:58:24.060 --> 00:58:26.700
but it's really
tough to get both.

00:58:26.700 --> 00:58:30.520
And we're optimizing
for the power,

00:58:30.520 --> 00:58:32.530
because we see a
giant hole there

00:58:32.530 --> 00:58:35.860
for running these
mission-critical custom apps.

00:58:35.860 --> 00:58:38.940
And what people are
doing needs more power,

00:58:38.940 --> 00:58:40.859
so that's what we're
optimizing for.

00:58:40.859 --> 00:58:42.150
Let's talk about manufacturing.

00:58:42.150 --> 00:58:44.410
How many of you have a
manufacturing background?

00:58:44.410 --> 00:58:46.020
Oh, that's great.

00:58:46.020 --> 00:58:47.520
I love manufacturing.

00:58:47.520 --> 00:58:48.930
And what kinds of things?

00:58:48.930 --> 00:58:51.780
What kinds of companies?

00:58:51.780 --> 00:58:52.680
You have--

00:58:52.680 --> 00:58:53.400
Pharmaceuticals.

00:58:53.400 --> 00:58:54.670
Pharmaceuticals?

00:58:54.670 --> 00:58:56.520
Any auto people here?

00:58:56.520 --> 00:58:57.020
Yeah.

00:58:57.020 --> 00:58:57.400
Him.

00:58:57.400 --> 00:58:57.910
Auto?

00:58:57.910 --> 00:58:59.100
Electronics?

00:58:59.100 --> 00:58:59.600
Which

00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:00.100
TI.

00:59:00.100 --> 00:59:00.812
TI, uh-huh.

00:59:00.812 --> 00:59:01.941
Motorola.

00:59:01.941 --> 00:59:02.440
Motorola?

00:59:02.440 --> 00:59:04.352
Is it true you're sending
your manufacturing

00:59:04.352 --> 00:59:05.770
overseas, or just--

00:59:05.770 --> 00:59:06.270
I heard--

00:59:06.270 --> 00:59:07.519
Yeah, I heard that rumor, too.

00:59:07.519 --> 00:59:10.640
No, it couldn't be
further from the truth.

00:59:10.640 --> 00:59:12.300
We love manufacturing at NeXT.

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:16.010
And when I was at Apple,
I had the good fortune

00:59:16.010 --> 00:59:18.860
to lead the effort to
build a Mac factory.

00:59:18.860 --> 00:59:21.680
And we designed, and built,
and operated that factory.

00:59:21.680 --> 00:59:23.145
And it was a real breakthrough.

00:59:23.145 --> 00:59:24.770
It was the best
factory in the industry

00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:26.404
until we built the at NeXT.

00:59:26.404 --> 00:59:27.320
And we made a lot of--

00:59:27.320 --> 00:59:28.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:28.580 --> 00:59:31.445
We made a lot of
mistakes, though.

00:59:31.445 --> 00:59:33.320
As an example, I remember
walking through it.

00:59:33.320 --> 00:59:34.861
You know, one of
the things you learn

00:59:34.861 --> 00:59:37.280
when you start building
factories is that warehouses

00:59:37.280 --> 00:59:39.050
are really bad, right?

00:59:39.050 --> 00:59:41.900
Warehouses are bad, because
you tend to put things in them.

00:59:41.900 --> 00:59:43.820
And inventory is really bad.

00:59:43.820 --> 00:59:47.104
Inventory is really bad,
because if it's defective,

00:59:47.104 --> 00:59:48.770
you don't find out
about it for a while.

00:59:48.770 --> 00:59:51.440
And you don't close the quality
feedback loop with the vendor,

00:59:51.440 --> 00:59:53.810
and correct the problem, until
they've made a zillion of them.

00:59:53.810 --> 00:59:55.430
What you want to do
is find the problem

00:59:55.430 --> 00:59:56.730
the first one that
comes in the door,

00:59:56.730 --> 00:59:59.104
and stop them from making more
until you fix the problem.

00:59:59.104 --> 01:00:02.142
So warehouses also cost money,
because you put all this stuff

01:00:02.142 --> 01:00:02.642
in them.

01:00:02.642 --> 01:00:04.250
And the stuff-- you have to
go borrow money from the bank,

01:00:04.250 --> 01:00:07.220
or use money that could be used
in a more productive purpose,

01:00:07.220 --> 01:00:09.380
so warehouses are bad.

01:00:09.380 --> 01:00:11.390
And you want to go to JIT.

01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:14.354
I'm sure you've studied this
all, and studied examples.

01:00:14.354 --> 01:00:16.270
I was walking through
the Mac factory one day,

01:00:16.270 --> 01:00:18.490
and the two biggest pieces
of automation we put in

01:00:18.490 --> 01:00:22.900
were a giant small-part
storage and retrieval system.

01:00:22.900 --> 01:00:24.940
It was the totes
that ran around.

01:00:24.940 --> 01:00:26.710
And the second one
was this giant burn

01:00:26.710 --> 01:00:28.930
in system at the end.

01:00:28.930 --> 01:00:32.290
And a few tens of millions of
dollars worth of equipment.

01:00:32.290 --> 01:00:36.340
And I realized, unfortunately
too late, that both of them

01:00:36.340 --> 01:00:38.210
are warehouses.

01:00:38.210 --> 01:00:41.710
They're just
high-tech warehouses.

01:00:41.710 --> 01:00:44.800
And so when we
looked at NeXT, we

01:00:44.800 --> 01:00:48.910
said no warehouses of any kind.

01:00:48.910 --> 01:00:51.160
We have a true JIT factory.

01:00:51.160 --> 01:00:53.830
Stuff comes in, and is delivered
right to the point of use

01:00:53.830 --> 01:00:54.850
on the factory floor.

01:00:54.850 --> 01:00:57.190
There is no warehouse.

01:00:57.190 --> 01:01:00.040
Deliveries are made daily,
sometimes more frequently

01:01:00.040 --> 01:01:01.150
than that.

01:01:01.150 --> 01:01:04.240
There is no outgoing warehouse.

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:06.380
Everything is visible.

01:01:06.380 --> 01:01:14.620
And the reason that we were able
to do a lot of what we've done

01:01:14.620 --> 01:01:17.529
= because we looked at-- well,
I'll give you an example.

01:01:17.529 --> 01:01:19.570
When we were learning
about manufacturing at Mac,

01:01:19.570 --> 01:01:21.789
we hired a Stanford
Business School Professor

01:01:21.789 --> 01:01:23.830
at the time named Steven
Wheelwright, who Harvard

01:01:23.830 --> 01:01:26.110
has since stolen away, I think.

01:01:26.110 --> 01:01:27.250
And he did a neat thing.

01:01:27.250 --> 01:01:29.720
He drew on the board a little
chart, first time I met him.

01:01:29.720 --> 01:01:32.590
He said, you can
view all companies

01:01:32.590 --> 01:01:34.870
from a manufacturing
perspective this way.

01:01:34.870 --> 01:01:37.390
You can say there's
five stages--

01:01:37.390 --> 01:01:39.075
one, two, three, four, five.

01:01:39.075 --> 01:01:40.200
They all have these things.

01:01:40.200 --> 01:01:44.080
And stage one is companies
that view manufacturing

01:01:44.080 --> 01:01:46.450
as a necessary evil.

01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:49.910
They wish they didn't have to
do it, but damn it, they do.

01:01:49.910 --> 01:01:52.240
And all the way up
through stage five,

01:01:52.240 --> 01:01:54.580
which is companies
that view manufacturing

01:01:54.580 --> 01:01:58.920
as a competitive opportunity for
competitive advantage, right?

01:01:58.920 --> 01:02:00.295
We can get better
time to market,

01:02:00.295 --> 01:02:01.628
and get new products out faster.

01:02:01.628 --> 01:02:02.480
We get lower costs.

01:02:02.480 --> 01:02:03.610
We get higher quality.

01:02:03.610 --> 01:02:05.800
And in general, you
know, you can, sort of,

01:02:05.800 --> 01:02:11.138
put the American flag here,
and put the Japanese flag here.

01:02:11.138 --> 01:02:12.572
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:12.572 --> 01:02:14.490
[APPLAUSE]

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:16.260
And that's changing, however.

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:17.220
That's changing.

01:02:17.220 --> 01:02:19.020
And it's changing,
because people like you

01:02:19.020 --> 01:02:20.760
are going into manufacturing.

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:22.754
Companies are starting
to realize that we

01:02:22.754 --> 01:02:23.920
were great at this one time.

01:02:23.920 --> 01:02:26.220
And then we took it for granted.

01:02:26.220 --> 01:02:29.190
And people are starting to
pay good salaries now, and get

01:02:29.190 --> 01:02:31.160
good people.

01:02:31.160 --> 01:02:34.530
And so we want to
be one of these.

01:02:34.530 --> 01:02:36.722
And we try very hard.

01:02:36.722 --> 01:02:38.930
By the way, just going back
to software for a minute,

01:02:38.930 --> 01:02:41.850
I often apply this scale
to computer companies,

01:02:41.850 --> 01:02:43.800
and how they look at software.

01:02:43.800 --> 01:02:46.380
See, I think most computer
companies are stage one.

01:02:46.380 --> 01:02:49.070
They wish software had
never been invented.

01:02:49.070 --> 01:02:51.030
I put Compaq in that category.

01:02:51.030 --> 01:02:53.925
And IBM is maybe stage
two, and things like that.

01:02:53.925 --> 01:02:56.020
And I think there's only--

01:02:56.020 --> 01:02:58.520
you know, Suns maybe, sort
of, in the middle, maybe here.

01:02:58.520 --> 01:03:00.270
I think there's only
three companies here,

01:03:00.270 --> 01:03:04.500
and that's us, Apple, and
Microsoft, in stage five.

01:03:04.500 --> 01:03:06.820
We start everything with
the software and work back.

01:03:06.820 --> 01:03:09.090
But anyway going back
to manufacturing,

01:03:09.090 --> 01:03:15.887
we started looking at the
factory as a software problem.

01:03:15.887 --> 01:03:17.720
And the first people
we hired in the factory

01:03:17.720 --> 01:03:19.160
were some software engineers.

01:03:19.160 --> 01:03:21.620
We convinced them to move
from R&amp;D into software,

01:03:21.620 --> 01:03:22.694
which was not easy.

01:03:22.694 --> 01:03:23.860
We had to give them bonuses.

01:03:23.860 --> 01:03:24.809
We had to cajole them.

01:03:24.809 --> 01:03:26.600
We had to promise them
they could come back

01:03:26.600 --> 01:03:27.479
if they hated it.

01:03:27.479 --> 01:03:28.520
And they went over there.

01:03:28.520 --> 01:03:31.160
And we said, this is really
just a software problem

01:03:31.160 --> 01:03:33.962
with interesting I/O
devices called robots.

01:03:33.962 --> 01:03:34.670
That's all it is.

01:03:34.670 --> 01:03:37.130
And so we started building
the software first.

01:03:37.130 --> 01:03:42.710
And our first robots that
we got, we specced them out.

01:03:42.710 --> 01:03:45.362
And we bought them
completely turnkey,

01:03:45.362 --> 01:03:47.570
with the robot arms on them,
and all the electronics,

01:03:47.570 --> 01:03:51.030
and the software
to control them.

01:03:51.030 --> 01:03:54.000
And we specced it out,
but we didn't write it.

01:03:54.000 --> 01:03:55.550
And they didn't--
they worked OK.

01:03:55.550 --> 01:03:58.550
Some of them are still in
use, but they weren't great.

01:03:58.550 --> 01:04:02.510
And being software folks,
we weren't real happy.

01:04:02.510 --> 01:04:03.440
They weren't elegant.

01:04:03.440 --> 01:04:05.030
We couldn't do what we
wanted with the robots.

01:04:05.030 --> 01:04:07.550
We couldn't tie-in a quality
information system to them,

01:04:07.550 --> 01:04:09.008
and all this other
stuff we wanted.

01:04:09.008 --> 01:04:11.570
So the second generation,
we specced out the hardware,

01:04:11.570 --> 01:04:13.550
and had somebody build
the hardware for us,

01:04:13.550 --> 01:04:16.520
but we wrote all the software
on our own computers.

01:04:16.520 --> 01:04:19.100
We're object-oriented, so we
started writing robot objects,

01:04:19.100 --> 01:04:21.980
quality objects, you
know, all sorts of objects

01:04:21.980 --> 01:04:23.840
to control this factory.

01:04:23.840 --> 01:04:26.270
And we found in our
computer was great for it.

01:04:26.270 --> 01:04:27.920
And so our whole
factory now runs

01:04:27.920 --> 01:04:32.240
on this object-oriented
factory and quality system.

01:04:32.240 --> 01:04:34.800
The last generation of-- our
latest generation of robots,

01:04:34.800 --> 01:04:38.580
which we've deployed this year,
we actually built the hardware.

01:04:38.580 --> 01:04:41.460
I've been to Japan maybe--
oh, a lot of times-- maybe 30,

01:04:41.460 --> 01:04:42.350
40 times.

01:04:42.350 --> 01:04:44.384
And I love to have
factories over there.

01:04:44.384 --> 01:04:45.800
They always amaze
me, because they

01:04:45.800 --> 01:04:47.660
built everything themselves.

01:04:47.660 --> 01:04:49.030
They weren't afraid of anything.

01:04:49.030 --> 01:04:49.940
They needed a robot.

01:04:49.940 --> 01:04:50.660
They tried to buy one.

01:04:50.660 --> 01:04:51.980
But if they couldn't,
they'd actually

01:04:51.980 --> 01:04:53.021
engineer it and build it.

01:04:53.021 --> 01:04:54.900
And you'd think this
was really expensive,

01:04:54.900 --> 01:04:57.710
but we found out
it's pretty cheap.

01:04:57.710 --> 01:04:59.704
It's actually cheaper
than buying them.

01:04:59.704 --> 01:05:01.370
And so we've actually
now designed our--

01:05:01.370 --> 01:05:02.630
and specced out our own robots.

01:05:02.630 --> 01:05:03.970
We don't mill the
metal or anything.

01:05:03.970 --> 01:05:04.520
We get that all made.

01:05:04.520 --> 01:05:05.660
We put them all together.

01:05:05.660 --> 01:05:07.370
And we do the software
top-to-bottom.

01:05:07.370 --> 01:05:10.400
And we have now some
extraordinarily advanced robots

01:05:10.400 --> 01:05:11.420
in the factory.

01:05:11.420 --> 01:05:14.010
And our computers are
built, start to finish,

01:05:14.010 --> 01:05:17.150
on the key components,
completely untouched

01:05:17.150 --> 01:05:18.770
by human hands.

01:05:18.770 --> 01:05:22.640
So we're pretty convinced
we're the low-cost producer.

01:05:22.640 --> 01:05:26.630
We do it in Fremont, California,
right under our nose.

01:05:26.630 --> 01:05:30.410
And we export them to Japan,
and all sorts of other places.

01:05:30.410 --> 01:05:32.570
And Canon is our
partner in Japan.

01:05:32.570 --> 01:05:36.320
And they do very, very
thorough quality audits.

01:05:36.320 --> 01:05:38.540
And we're now at the
point where we're directly

01:05:38.540 --> 01:05:39.810
shipped to stock with them.

01:05:39.810 --> 01:05:42.070
And they say we're a very
high-quality supplier.

01:05:45.046 --> 01:05:47.014
How do your lines tie
in with your research--

01:05:47.014 --> 01:05:47.998
your development team?

01:05:47.998 --> 01:05:49.831
Because I had heard
that they could actually

01:05:49.831 --> 01:05:52.685
change the line from their
own computers [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:52.685 --> 01:05:53.310
Yeah, they can.

01:05:53.310 --> 01:05:55.030
Well, we don't give everybody
permission to do that, but

01:05:55.030 --> 01:05:55.530
[LAUGHTER]

01:05:55.530 --> 01:05:57.050
--yes, they can.

01:05:57.050 --> 01:05:58.160
Here's how it works.

01:05:58.160 --> 01:06:00.211
One of the things we
do is we actually--

01:06:00.211 --> 01:06:02.210
when we want to build an
engineering prototype--

01:06:02.210 --> 01:06:04.990
see what happens in most--

01:06:04.990 --> 01:06:06.820
one of the key things
that manufacturing

01:06:06.820 --> 01:06:09.830
can contribute to competitive
advantage is time to market.

01:06:09.830 --> 01:06:10.910
Why is that?

01:06:10.910 --> 01:06:12.700
Because the way
most things work is

01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:16.660
you design your product here.

01:06:16.660 --> 01:06:19.360
And after you're done, you
throw it over the wall.

01:06:19.360 --> 01:06:22.902
And you design your
manufacturing process here,

01:06:22.902 --> 01:06:25.360
sorting out a bunch of things
that maybe weren't done right

01:06:25.360 --> 01:06:27.568
here, fixing them, changing
them, and then completing

01:06:27.568 --> 01:06:28.960
the process design.

01:06:28.960 --> 01:06:31.960
What you want to do is
do this, and ship it

01:06:31.960 --> 01:06:34.720
right here while your
competitors are still here.

01:06:34.720 --> 01:06:37.550
And that's what we've been
able to do in many cases.

01:06:37.550 --> 01:06:41.260
What we do is, we suck
data out of our CAD systems

01:06:41.260 --> 01:06:42.520
in engineering.

01:06:42.520 --> 01:06:44.770
We zing them around over
the local networks over a T1

01:06:44.770 --> 01:06:47.560
to [INAUDIBLE] factory
is about 15 minutes away.

01:06:47.560 --> 01:06:51.500
And in our own
computers, we compute

01:06:51.500 --> 01:06:56.240
all of the robot placement
programs' fully-optimized path.

01:06:56.240 --> 01:06:58.610
We compute all the
vision system programs.

01:06:58.610 --> 01:07:02.060
We check it against the bill
of materials in the IS system.

01:07:02.060 --> 01:07:03.560
And we download
it to the robots.

01:07:03.560 --> 01:07:07.400
And we're ready to build
a board, lot size of one,

01:07:07.400 --> 01:07:10.430
in-between two production
CPU boards on the line,

01:07:10.430 --> 01:07:13.940
full surface mount with all
of our automation technology.

01:07:13.940 --> 01:07:19.040
Now, the key is that
manufacturing did that so well

01:07:19.040 --> 01:07:22.700
for engineering, that we
haven't built a prototype

01:07:22.700 --> 01:07:25.220
in engineering for two years.

01:07:25.220 --> 01:07:28.100
We haven't built a wire wrap,
or any other kind of prototype

01:07:28.100 --> 01:07:29.690
in engineering for two years.

01:07:29.690 --> 01:07:32.030
Everything has been
built in the factory.

01:07:32.030 --> 01:07:33.570
Now, what does that mean?

01:07:33.570 --> 01:07:36.080
What that means
is, manufacturing

01:07:36.080 --> 01:07:39.194
gets involved from day one.

01:07:39.194 --> 01:07:40.610
Because the-- the
engineering guys

01:07:40.610 --> 01:07:43.500
call up manufacturing go, hey,
we want to build a prototype.

01:07:43.500 --> 01:07:45.750
We're going to need these
special parts in that thing.

01:07:45.750 --> 01:07:46.440
Take a look at this.

01:07:46.440 --> 01:07:47.120
Tell us what you think.

01:07:47.120 --> 01:07:48.480
We'd like to do it tomorrow.

01:07:48.480 --> 01:07:50.271
Let us know if that's
OK, blah, blah, blah.

01:07:50.271 --> 01:07:51.620
They get involved from day one.

01:07:51.620 --> 01:07:53.825
And what it also means is--

01:07:53.825 --> 01:07:54.950
so we get this parallelism.

01:07:54.950 --> 01:07:59.660
Secondly, a lot of times,
when you build prototypes,

01:07:59.660 --> 01:08:01.430
it's not quite the
same technology

01:08:01.430 --> 01:08:03.390
as you're going to
use in production.

01:08:03.390 --> 01:08:05.157
And so all the
accumulated knowledge

01:08:05.157 --> 01:08:06.740
you get from building
your prototypes,

01:08:06.740 --> 01:08:09.080
you throw away when
you change technology

01:08:09.080 --> 01:08:10.220
to go into production.

01:08:10.220 --> 01:08:12.800
And you start over in
that accumulation process.

01:08:12.800 --> 01:08:15.980
Because we don't
change technology,

01:08:15.980 --> 01:08:19.170
we don't throw anything away.

01:08:19.170 --> 01:08:21.090
We don't waste time.

01:08:21.090 --> 01:08:24.007
And it's led to one of the
healthiest relationships

01:08:24.007 --> 01:08:26.340
between an engineering and
manufacturing group I've ever

01:08:26.340 --> 01:08:27.600
seen in my life.

01:08:27.600 --> 01:08:29.700
They're all working
off the same databases.

01:08:29.700 --> 01:08:32.399
They're all working
on the same processes.

01:08:32.399 --> 01:08:34.830
They're all working in a
very disciplined process

01:08:34.830 --> 01:08:38.040
environment, to where, when
any processes are change,

01:08:38.040 --> 01:08:41.520
they all get together
and review the proposals,

01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:42.770
and all buy into it.

01:08:42.770 --> 01:08:45.069
And it's not that hard.

01:08:45.069 --> 01:08:47.130
The key to it all,
though, was we

01:08:47.130 --> 01:08:50.580
didn't go out and hire a
bunch manufacturing people.

01:08:50.580 --> 01:08:52.910
We went out and hired engineers.

01:08:52.910 --> 01:08:55.395
And we convinced them that we
were going to be different.

01:08:55.395 --> 01:08:57.145
We were going to pay
them exactly the same

01:08:57.145 --> 01:08:58.728
as-- as a matter of
fact, we paid them

01:08:58.728 --> 01:09:00.050
a little more at the beginning.

01:09:00.050 --> 01:09:04.200
But we pay him exactly the
same as R&amp;D, no different.

01:09:04.200 --> 01:09:06.920
There's migration,
both directions,

01:09:06.920 --> 01:09:10.850
not just from manufacturing
into R&amp;D, but both directions.

01:09:10.850 --> 01:09:13.189
And they're not
second-class citizens.

01:09:13.189 --> 01:09:14.569
They have the same offices.

01:09:14.569 --> 01:09:16.069
They have the same
test equipment.

01:09:16.069 --> 01:09:20.120
They have the same
computers on their desks.

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:21.800
And it took us a
while to convince them

01:09:21.800 --> 01:09:25.290
that we were really serious.

01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:27.350
For about the first few
years, we had more PhDs

01:09:27.350 --> 01:09:29.350
in manufacturing than we
did design engineering,

01:09:29.350 --> 01:09:31.850
until design engineering
stole a few of them away.

01:09:31.850 --> 01:09:34.250
So it's really paid
off for us, and I

01:09:34.250 --> 01:09:36.859
think it's one of our
real opportunities

01:09:36.859 --> 01:09:39.341
for competitive advantage.

01:09:39.341 --> 01:09:42.045
Yeah, I think one or two
more, and we've got to run.

01:09:42.045 --> 01:09:43.229
And probably, you do too.

01:09:43.229 --> 01:09:44.354
Yes, up there, [INAUDIBLE]?

01:09:44.354 --> 01:09:46.126
So you have no
warehouses, is that true?

01:09:46.126 --> 01:09:48.360
You are doing true JIT?

01:09:48.360 --> 01:09:48.859
[INAUDIBLE]

01:09:48.859 --> 01:09:49.359
Yeah.

01:09:50.104 --> 01:09:53.794
How are you getting your
products or your raw materials?

01:09:53.794 --> 01:09:55.335
How are we getting
our raw materials?

01:09:55.335 --> 01:09:55.980
Yeah.

01:09:55.980 --> 01:09:58.429
You mean, like, what
truck line brings them in?

01:09:58.429 --> 01:09:59.262
Or what do you mean?

01:09:59.262 --> 01:10:01.722
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:02.698 --> 01:10:03.198
Is it air?

01:10:03.198 --> 01:10:03.698
Is it truck?

01:10:03.698 --> 01:10:06.150
I mean, how are you getting
everything in that quickly?

01:10:06.150 --> 01:10:09.300
See the key thing is,
that's not our problem.

01:10:09.300 --> 01:10:11.370
That's our suppliers' problem.

01:10:11.370 --> 01:10:14.220
So we agree with our supplier
when the stuff is going

01:10:14.220 --> 01:10:16.800
to arrive on our factory floor.

01:10:16.800 --> 01:10:19.860
And if they can-- if they're
together enough to ship it

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:22.090
by truck, that's fine.

01:10:22.090 --> 01:10:25.677
If they have to ship it
by air, that's too bad.

01:10:25.677 --> 01:10:28.260
If they want to have a warehouse
next to ours, because they're

01:10:28.260 --> 01:10:31.290
not good enough, well,
then they have to do that.

01:10:31.290 --> 01:10:33.060
Now, we're not
giant, so we can't

01:10:33.060 --> 01:10:34.890
go command people to do things.

01:10:34.890 --> 01:10:38.280
But what's happened is, is we
have a fairly narrow supply

01:10:38.280 --> 01:10:38.910
base.

01:10:38.910 --> 01:10:41.850
We don't have three
billion suppliers.

01:10:41.850 --> 01:10:45.060
And they see tremendous
advantages in working with us.

01:10:45.060 --> 01:10:47.470
We're pushing our quality
information systems back

01:10:47.470 --> 01:10:47.970
to them.

01:10:47.970 --> 01:10:50.136
As an example, Motorola is
one of our key suppliers.

01:10:50.136 --> 01:10:52.530
Almost every key supplier
has NeXT computers.

01:10:52.530 --> 01:10:55.740
And we send them statistical
quality information,

01:10:55.740 --> 01:10:58.402
sometimes daily--

01:10:58.402 --> 01:11:01.610
daily-- off our automated
quality information systems

01:11:01.610 --> 01:11:02.960
on their parts.

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:06.300
And those kinds of things are
extremely valuable to them.

01:11:06.300 --> 01:11:09.170
So while we're
not Goliath, we're

01:11:09.170 --> 01:11:11.670
a very valuable
David to work with.

01:11:11.670 --> 01:11:14.330
And so they really bend over
backwards to work with us.

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:18.770
And we try to push the
problems where they belong.

01:11:18.770 --> 01:11:21.500
If it's our problems, we take
full responsibility for them.

01:11:21.500 --> 01:11:23.300
We own our process.

01:11:23.300 --> 01:11:28.220
But they-- it's their job to
get us a zero defect material

01:11:28.220 --> 01:11:30.335
on-time, per agreements.

01:11:30.335 --> 01:11:32.960
And our philosophy is, our money
doesn't break after we give it

01:11:32.960 --> 01:11:34.640
to them, so their
parts shouldn't break

01:11:34.640 --> 01:11:35.764
after they give them to us.

01:11:35.764 --> 01:11:37.346
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:37.346 --> 01:11:39.741
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:44.300 --> 01:11:44.800
Yeah?

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:47.445
Do you see any possibility
of bringing NeXT over

01:11:47.445 --> 01:11:50.091
to [INAUDIBLE] portable Macs?

01:11:50.091 --> 01:11:52.496
And do you see the future
of Macs [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:52.496 --> 01:11:55.400
dying out, or what [INAUDIBLE]?

01:11:55.400 --> 01:11:57.890
You know, I think Macs are
going to continue to grow.

01:11:57.890 --> 01:11:59.840
It depends on what Apple does.

01:11:59.840 --> 01:12:01.610
I think certain segments
of the Mac market

01:12:01.610 --> 01:12:03.870
are going to continue to grow.

01:12:03.870 --> 01:12:07.330
I think certain segments
of the Mac market

01:12:07.330 --> 01:12:12.310
are not going to be
targeted for future growth.

01:12:12.310 --> 01:12:17.300
And I think Macs are good
computers to do certain things.

01:12:17.300 --> 01:12:19.300
But to do the things
that people I think

01:12:19.300 --> 01:12:21.416
are going to want to
do in a few years,

01:12:21.416 --> 01:12:23.040
I don't think you
can do them on a Mac.

01:12:23.040 --> 01:12:26.840
I don't think you can do
them on DOS and Windows.

01:12:26.840 --> 01:12:30.020
And so I think there's a
need for some new technology.

01:12:30.020 --> 01:12:33.650
Just like, you know, there a
lot of things that you can do

01:12:33.650 --> 01:12:37.070
on DOS when the Mac came out,
but there were some new things

01:12:37.070 --> 01:12:37.820
you just couldn't.

01:12:37.820 --> 01:12:39.070
And it would take a long time.

01:12:39.070 --> 01:12:41.354
And I think it's the
same way with the Mac.

01:12:41.354 --> 01:12:43.020
Hey, we have to head
off into the sunset

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:45.240
back to California,
but thanks for a chance

01:12:45.240 --> 01:12:47.200
to be with you
all for some time.

01:12:47.200 --> 01:12:50.250
[APPLAUSE]

